OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026", "title": "Generic C1 Future Inextendibility Near Rotating Subextremal Kerr Spacetimes", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "264", "family_title": "Strong cosmic censorship near two-ended Kerr data", "abstract_html": "For each fixed Kerr spacetime with mass <i>M</i> &amp;gt; 0 and rotation \\(0\\lt \\mathfrak a\\lt M\\), we prove that a dense <i>G</i><sub><i>δ</i></sub> set of nearby smooth, complete two-ended vacuum data has a maximal globally hyperbolic development with no future <i>C</i><sup>1</sup> extension. The neighborhood and genericity are defined in a weighted smooth topology, and ambient extensions may be nonvacuum.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 2.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is \\(\\varepsilon_0=\\varepsilon_0(M,a)>0\\) such that \\(\\mathcal U_{\\varepsilon_0}\\) contains a dense \\(G_\\delta\\) subset \\(\\mathcal G\\) for which every \\(\\mathcal M_d\\), \\(d\\in\\mathcal G\\), has no future \\(C^1\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Lorentzian signature \\((-+++)\\). Fix a Kerr mass \\(M&gt;0\\) and rotation parameter \\(0&lt;a&lt;M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(d_*=(h_*,K_*)\\) be the complete signed Killing-time-zero Kerr bridge through the bifurcation sphere, with the future-normal convention of [20, Sections 1 and 8], on\n  \\[\n  \\Sigma\\simeq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb S^2.\n  \\]\n  Fix its identification with \\(\\Sigma\\), asymptotically Cartesian charts on both ends, and a smooth function \\(\\rho\\ge1\\) equal to the end radius outside a compact set. Let \\(\\nabla_*\\) and \\(|\\cdot|_*\\) denote the reference connection and norm.</p><p class=\"li\">For smooth symmetric two-tensors \\(f,j\\), define the increasing seminorms\n  \\[\n  p_m(f,j)=\\sum_{i=0}^m\\left\\{\n  \\sup_\\Sigma\\rho^{1+i}|\\nabla_*^if|_*\n  +\\sup_\\Sigma\\rho^{2+i}|\\nabla_*^ij|_*\n  \\right\\},\\qquad m=0,1,\\ldots.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal D\\) consist of smooth pairs \\(d=(h,K)\\) for which \\(h\\) is positive definite and complete, every \\(p_m(h-h_*,K-K_*)\\) is finite, and\n  \\[\n  R(h)+(\\operatorname{tr}_hK)^2-|K|_h^2=0,\\qquad\n  \\operatorname{div}_hK-d(\\operatorname{tr}_hK)=0.\n  \\]\n  Give \\(\\mathcal D\\) the relative topology of these seminorms, and set\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal U_\\varepsilon\n  =\\{d\\in\\mathcal D:p_{10}(d-d_*)&lt;\\varepsilon\\}.\n  \\]\n  These are differentiated \\(O(r^{-1})\\) metric and \\(O(r^{-2})\\) second-form conditions. No parity, fixed leading coefficient, fixed charge, or symmetry restriction is imposed. Representatives are used in the fixed spatial gauge rather than modulo diffeomorphisms.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal M_d\\) be the full smooth maximal globally hyperbolic vacuum development of \\(d\\), with its prescribed time orientation.</p><p class=\"li\">A future \\(C^1\\) extension means a smooth, time-orientation-preserving isometric embedding\n  \\[\n  \\iota:\\mathcal M_d\\longrightarrow\\widetilde M\n  \\]\n  onto a proper open subset of a connected, time-oriented smooth four-manifold carrying a nondegenerate \\(C^1\\) Lorentzian metric \\(\\widetilde g\\), together with a future-directed timelike \\(C^1\\) curve \\(c:[0,1]\\to\\widetilde M\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  c([0,1))\\subset\\iota(\\mathcal M_d),\\qquad\n  c(1)\\in\\partial\\iota(\\mathcal M_d).\n  \\]\n  Neither the vacuum equations nor global hyperbolicity are imposed on the ambient spacetime.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(\\varepsilon_0=\\varepsilon_0(M,a)&gt;0\\) such that \\(\\mathcal U_{\\varepsilon_0}\\) contains a dense \\(G_\\delta\\) subset \\(\\mathcal G\\) for which every \\(\\mathcal M_d\\), \\(d\\in\\mathcal G\\), has no future \\(C^1\\) extension.</p><p>The theorem does not assert that inextendibility is open or that every datum in \\(\\mathcal U_{\\varepsilon_0}\\) is inextendible. Its constants need not be uniform as \\(a\\to0\\) or \\(a\\to M\\). Only \\(p_{10}\\) is required small; higher seminorms are finite but need not be uniformly small.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Proposition 1, reduce to the normalized fixed center \\(M=1\\), \\(0&lt;a&lt;1\\), and work in the sufficiently small nonempty Baire neighborhood \\(\\mathcal U\\).</p><p>Proposition 2 makes every passing set \\(\\mathcal A(O,B)\\) relatively closed. Proposition 16 makes its relative interior empty: every relative neighborhood contains a finite \\(p_m\\)-ball, and its radius may be reduced to retain the positive slack in the defining strict \\(p_{10}\\)-inequality. A sufficiently late signed experiment then gives a datum in that ball outside the passing set.</p><p>The basis \\(\\mathscr O\\) and integers \\(B\\ge1\\) are countable. Hence\n\\[\n\\mathcal G\n=\\bigcap_{\\substack{O\\in\\mathscr O\\\\B\\in\\mathbb N,\\ B\\ge1}}\n\\bigl(\\mathcal U\\setminus\\mathcal A(O,B)\\bigr)\n\\]\nis a dense \\(G_\\delta\\) in \\(\\mathcal U\\).</p><p>Every datum admitting a future \\(C^1\\) extension belongs to a passing set by Proposition 2. No datum in \\(\\mathcal G\\) admits one. The tests and finite loops concern the full maximal development, and ambient extensions were not assumed vacuum.</p><p>The inverse mass normalization of Proposition 1 proves the result for arbitrary fixed \\(M&gt;0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 5.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After fixing the required finite derivative buffers, choosing \\(\\zeta>0\\) sufficiently small, and then choosing sufficiently large finite inverse-accuracy and formal orders, there are smooth positive complete vacuum …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in the weighted constraint space of Theorem 1, after the mass normalization \\(M=1\\), near a fixed Kerr center with \\(0&lt;a&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a smooth datum \\(d\\) in the sufficiently small \\(p_{10}\\)-neighborhood used for the comparison geometry. Fix a noncorner branch of its comparison development, prepared so that \\(v\\to\\infty\\) and \\(u\\to u_*&lt;\\infty\\).</p><p class=\"li\">On the interior wedge write\n  \\[\n  g=-2a\\,du\\,dv+\\gamma_{AB}(d\\theta^A-b^Adu)(d\\theta^B-b^Bdu),\n  \\qquad\n  a=qA,\\quad q=q_0e^{-\\kappa(u+v)}.\n  \\]\n  In this statement \\(a\\) is the optical metric coefficient, not the Kerr rotation parameter. Put\n  \\[\n  L_0=\\partial_u+b^A\\partial_A,\\quad L_1=\\partial_v,\\quad\n  \\widehat e_i=a^{-1/2}L_i,\\quad \\widehat e_A=\\partial_A.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\), and put\n  \\[\n  A_T=e^{-\\alpha T},\\qquad \\lambda_T=e^{\\zeta T},\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\) will be chosen sufficiently small.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the linear interior packets \\(h_T^p\\) and exterior beams \\(h_T^B\\) of Proposition 8, with this amplitude, and let \\(q_T\\) be the relative array of \\(h_T^p\\) in the displayed normalized frame.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a finite data order \\(m\\ge10\\), a finite entry order \\(r\\), a loss \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), and \\(0&lt;\\nu_0&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S_T=S\\cap\\mathcal D_T\\), where \\(S=\\{t=(u+v)/2=0\\}\\) and\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal D_T=\n  \\left\\{\n  0\\le t\\le \\frac{T+u_*}{2}+c_0,\\quad\n  u+\\frac{c_0t}{T}\\le u_2,\\quad\n  v+\\frac{c_0t}{T}\\le T+c_2\n  \\right\\},\n  \\]\n  with fixed \\(u_2&gt;u_*\\), \\(c_2&gt;0\\), and sufficiently small \\(c_0&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a tensor \\(W\\) defined on an entry collar, let \\(\\widehat W\\) be its normalized array and define the full spacetime entry-jet norm\n  \\[\n  \\mathfrak J_{r,T}(W)=\n  \\sum_{|I|\\le r}\n  \\left(\n  \\|D^I\\widehat W|_{S_T}\\|_{L^\\infty}\n  +\\|D^I\\widehat W|_{S_T}\\|_{L^2(dx\\,d\\operatorname{vol}_\\gamma)}\n  \\right).\n  \\]\n  The derivatives include derivatives normal to \\(S\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After fixing the required finite derivative buffers, choosing \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\) sufficiently small, and then choosing sufficiently large finite inverse-accuracy and formal orders, there are smooth positive complete vacuum data \\(d_T^\\pm\\in\\mathcal D\\) on the entire original bridge such that\n\\[\np_m(d_T^\\pm-d)\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThey agree exactly with \\(d\\) on the other end, the connecting region, and an expanding protected region containing every fixed compact subset. On the selected end the protected ball is\n\\[\nB_T=\\{|z-z_*|&lt;\\rho_T\\},\\qquad\nz_*=C_e(t_T),\\qquad\n\\rho_T=(1-2\\beta)t_T,\n\\]\nfor sufficiently small fixed \\(\\beta&gt;0\\). Their full correction tails are retained and have the differentiated weight-one metric and weight-two second-form bounds at every order.</p><p>Their ordinary exterior vacuum evolutions \\(g_T^\\pm\\), in exact wave-map gauge with the same target \\(g\\), exist through the needed entry collar with the fixed overlap and strict future-outflow margins. Writing\n\\[\nH_T^\\pm=\\widehat{g_T^\\pm-g},\\qquad\nF_T^\\pm=H_T^\\pm\\mp q_T,\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n\\mathfrak J_{r,T}(F_T^\\pm)\\le A_T^2e^{\\eta T},\\qquad\n\\mathfrak J_{r,T}(F_T^+-F_T^-)\\le A_T^3e^{\\eta T}.\n\\]\nThe exact changes, packets, and errors vanish on the needed entry interval \\(v&lt;(1-\\nu_0)T\\). Any prescribed finite linear deep-residual bound, including \\(e^{-4\\kappa T}\\) through order \\(60\\), can be imposed simultaneously.</p><p>This statement ends at the entry cylinder; it asserts no general-amplitude interior comparison estimate.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 8, including its original Corollary 4.8 conclusion, gives the linear construction for every fixed \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\). Repeat the signed preparation argument of Proposition 9 at that amplitude.</p><p>The rescaled constraint solve and odd estimate require only\n\\[\nA_T\\lambda_T^P\\to0\n\\]\nfor finitely many fixed positive powers \\(P\\). Choose internal losses smaller than the prescribed \\(\\eta\\) and a sufficiently small fixed fraction of \\(\\alpha\\). The exterior bootstrap improves because\n\\[\nA_T^2e^{\\eta_{\\mathrm{int}}T}=o(A_T).\n\\]\nThe common integrated exterior rate can be made arbitrarily small independently of \\(\\alpha\\), by the same far-radius and first-arrival choices. The finite triangular propagator preserves that rate and the cubic odd gain.</p><p>Choose \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\) so every fixed positive-frequency loss \\(P\\zeta\\) fits its allocated budget and is smaller than \\(\\alpha\\). After fixing a positive remaining margin \\(\\delta\\), choose finite inverse accuracy with\n\\[\n\\alpha+M_1\\zeta&gt;\\max\\{3\\alpha,4\\kappa\\}+\\delta.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nA_T\\lambda_T^{-M_1}e^{o(T)}\n\\]\nis smaller than both cubic amplitude and \\(e^{-4\\kappa T}\\). All-order smoothness comes from the same base-order correction, and all support and tail statements are unchanged.</p><p>This proves the general-amplitude signed exterior preparation. No interior comparison estimate is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Lemma A.1; the nearby-center assertion is the unnumbered observation following its proof", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every neighborhood of \\(d_*\\) in \\(\\mathcal D\\) contains complete vacuum data whose spatial data isometry group is trivial.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(M&gt;0\\), \\(0&lt;a&lt;M\\), and the Kerr bridge datum \\(d_*=(h_*,K_*)\\) in the weighted constraint space \\(\\mathcal D\\) of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">A spatial data isometry is a diffeomorphism of \\(\\Sigma\\) preserving both \\(h\\) and \\(K\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every neighborhood of \\(d_*\\) in \\(\\mathcal D\\) contains complete vacuum data whose spatial data isometry group is trivial.</p><p>In addition, suitable smooth identifications of bridges place all sufficiently nearby Kerr centers in this same weighted phase space. The trivial-spatial-isometry examples constructed here are hypersurfaces in Kerr; this assertion does not remove Kerr’s spacetime Killing fields.</p>", "proof": "<p>The reference slice is maximal, \\(\\operatorname{tr}_{h_*}K_*=0\\). Simultaneous reversal of Killing time and azimuth preserves the spatial-data convention while reversing this axisymmetric scalar.</p><p>A compactly supported small spacelike normal graph in smooth Kerr gives exact vacuum constraint data identical to \\(d_*\\) off a compact set. The first variation of its second-form trace has principal part \\(\\pm\\Delta_{h_*}\\), plus smooth lower-order terms.</p><p>Choose a small normal-coordinate ball and heights\n\\[\nf_\\ell(z)=\\ell^2f_0(z/\\ell).\n\\]\nAfter rescaling to a fixed ball, the linearized trace converges smoothly as \\(\\ell\\to0\\) to \\(\\pm\\Delta_{\\mathbb R^3}f_0\\). Choose compactly supported \\(f_0\\) so this function has a unique strict positive global maximum, nondegenerate Hessian with simple spectrum, and nonzero third derivative in each Hessian eigendirection. The paper’s direct construction begins with a negative Gaussian with three distinct, sufficiently close axis scales; its Laplacian has a unique strict maximum and negative simple-spectrum Hessian. Cut it off sufficiently far away, where derivatives are small, and add arbitrarily small cutoff fifth powers in the three directions. Their Laplacians supply the required cubic derivatives while preserving the maximum and Hessian properties.</p><p>First take \\(\\ell\\) small, then the actual graph amplitude small. The trace divided by amplitude retains these properties relative to the induced metric; rescaled metric coefficients converge to Euclidean ones. Outside the patch the trace is zero, below the selected positive maximum.</p><p>Every data isometry fixes the unique maximum. Its differential preserves the simple Hessian eigenspaces. Nonzero odd third derivatives exclude every sign flip, so the differential is identity. An isometry of a connected Riemannian manifold is determined by its value and differential at one point; therefore it is identity.</p><p>The graph is unchanged outside a compact set and complete. Taking its amplitude small gives convergence in any prescribed finite collection of seminorms, hence examples in every neighborhood. They remain hypersurfaces in Kerr and do not eliminate its spacetime Killing fields.</p><p>For nearby centers, identify bridges near the waist using a signed square root of \\(r-r_+(M,a)\\). The Boyer–Lindquist radial pole then becomes a smooth positive radial metric coefficient. The azimuthal-shift angular velocity is smooth in \\(r,\\theta\\) and constant on the horizon sphere; dividing its metric Lie derivative by the signed lapse gives a smooth second form across the waist. End formulas have parameter-smooth symbol estimates with fixed Euclidean leading metric. These identifications place nearby Kerr centers in the same phase space.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemma 2.4, together with the topology and normalization observations in Section 2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The space \\(\\mathcal D\\) is completely metrizable, and every \\(\\mathcal U_\\varepsilon\\) is a nonempty Baire space.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(\\mathcal D\\), \\(p_m\\), \\(d_*\\), and \\(\\mathcal U_\\varepsilon\\) as defined in Theorem 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The space \\(\\mathcal D\\) is completely metrizable, and every \\(\\mathcal U_\\varepsilon\\) is a nonempty Baire space. Every neighborhood of any datum contains a finite seminorm ball\n\\[\n\\{d'\\in\\mathcal D:p_m(d'-d)&lt;r\\}\n\\]\nfor some finite \\(m\\ge10\\) and \\(r&gt;0\\).</p><p>Equivalent end charts, weights, and reference geometries give the same topology under the paper’s stated symbol and leading-Euclidean-metric conditions.</p><p>The constant rescaling \\(g\\mapsto M^{-2}g\\), in dimensionless bridge coordinates, induces\n\\[\n(h,K)\\mapsto(M^{-2}h,M^{-1}K),\n\\]\ngives a homeomorphism of the corresponding weighted constraint spaces, sends the center to \\((1,a/M)\\), and preserves maximality and the future-extension definition.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal E\\) be the vector space of tensor pairs for which every \\(p_m\\) is finite. It is complete for the usual metric constructed from the countable increasing seminorms. A Cauchy sequence converges smoothly on compact sets, and its weighted uniform derivative limits agree with the derivatives of the local smooth limit. Passing the uniform Cauchy bounds to the limit gives convergence in every \\(p_m\\).</p><p>If \\(h=h_*+f&gt;0\\) and \\(p_0(f,0)&lt;\\infty\\), decay makes \\(h\\) uniformly comparable with \\(h_*\\) on the ends. Positivity on the remaining compact set gives a global comparison, hence completeness. The same positive lower comparison bound makes positivity open in \\(\\mathcal E\\). Thus the explicit completeness condition is automatic on this positive open set.</p><p>The constraint map is continuous into smooth scalars and one-forms with their local smooth topology. Its zero locus is relatively closed. An open subset of a completely metrizable space is completely metrizable, as is a closed subset of it. This proves the assertion for \\(\\mathcal D\\) and its open subsets \\(\\mathcal U_\\varepsilon\\), which contain \\(d_*\\). Since the seminorms increase, finitely many seminorm inequalities follow from one sufficiently high-order inequality with sufficiently small radius.</p><p>For the topology assertion, assume comparable radii, transition Jacobians and inverse Jacobians that are symbols of order zero, and transformed reference geometries in the same asymptotic class. The tensor chain rule and the connection-change formula bound each seminorm by finitely many seminorms in the other system, in both directions. The leading Euclidean metric must be preserved up to the allowed metric-weight error. A smooth reidentification carrying both datum and reference center is therefore a homeomorphism. This does not identify seminorms under arbitrary data-dependent gauge transformations.</p><p>The stated rescaling gives the displayed initial-data scaling, preserves the vacuum equations, maximality, and the existence of a future \\(C^1\\) extension, and is a homeomorphism for the corresponding weighted topologies. Hence it suffices below to work with \\(M=1\\). Fix \\(0&lt;a&lt;1\\), choose the defining \\(p_{10}\\)-radius small enough for the geometric inputs, and write \\(\\mathcal U=\\mathcal U_{\\varepsilon_0}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 3.7", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every \\(\\mathcal A(O,B)\\) is relatively closed in \\(\\mathcal U\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(d=(h,K)\\in\\mathcal U\\), its full development \\(\\mathcal M_d\\), and its future unit normal \\(n\\) along \\(\\Sigma\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A seed \\(w\\in T\\Sigma\\) includes its base point. Define\n  \\[\n  U_d(w)=\\sqrt{1+h(w,w)}\\,n+w,\n  \\qquad\n  \\gamma_{d,w}:[0,T_d(w))\\to\\mathcal M_d,\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\gamma_{d,w}\\) is the maximal future unit timelike geodesic and \\(T_d(w)\\in(0,\\infty]\\) is its full-development proper duration.</p><p class=\"li\">On \\(\\mathbb Rn\\oplus T\\Sigma\\), define\n  \\[\n  k_{d,w}(0)(sn+X,tn+Y)=st+h(X,Y),\n  \\qquad\n  \\nabla_{\\dot\\gamma_{d,w}}k_{d,w}=0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a piecewise smooth loop \\(\\ell\\) based at \\(\\gamma_{d,w}(\\tau)\\), parallel transport this positive metric along \\(\\ell\\), obtaining \\(k_\\ell(s)\\), and set\n  \\[\n  L(\\ell)=\\int_0^1|\\dot\\ell(s)|_{k_\\ell(s)}\\,ds,\n  \\qquad\n  P_\\ell:T_{\\ell(0)}\\mathcal M_d\\to T_{\\ell(0)}\\mathcal M_d.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a countable basis \\(\\mathscr O\\) of nonempty relatively compact open subsets of \\(T\\Sigma\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(O\\in\\mathscr O\\) and an integer \\(B\\ge1\\), let \\(\\mathcal A(O,B)\\subset\\mathcal U\\) consist of those data such that every \\(w\\in O\\) has \\(T_d(w)\\le B\\), and, for every \\(0&lt;\\tau&lt;T_d(w)\\) and every piecewise smooth loop based at \\(\\gamma_{d,w}(\\tau)\\) with image in \\(I^+_{\\mathcal M_d}(\\Sigma)\\),\n  \\[\n  L(\\ell)&lt;B^{-1}\n  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n  \\|P_\\ell-\\operatorname{Id}\\|_{k_{d,w}(\\tau)}\n  \\le BL(\\ell).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every \\(\\mathcal A(O,B)\\) is relatively closed in \\(\\mathcal U\\). If \\(\\mathcal M_d\\) admits a future \\(C^1\\) extension, then\n\\[\nd\\in\\mathcal A(O,B)\n\\]\nfor some \\(O\\in\\mathscr O\\) and some integer \\(B\\ge1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Closedness is Lemma 2.2. For coverage, identify \\(\\mathcal M_d\\) with its open image in a hypothetical extension. The exiting curve has no interior future endpoint, since an interior limit and its ambient boundary limit would coincide in a Hausdorff manifold. Maximal past continuation and the Cauchy property put a sufficiently late tail in \\(I^+(\\Sigma)\\).</p><p>First obtain a uniformly timelike straight segment reaching a boundary point. Choose a precompact ambient chart \\((t,x)\\) containing the endpoint and a tail, with \\(dt\\) temporal and \\(\\partial_t\\) future timelike. Write the tail as \\((t,x(t))\\), \\(t\\le t_f\\). On a smaller compact box the causal slopes are bounded and\n\\[\n\\widetilde g((1,v),\\partial_t)\\le-c_0&lt;0.\n\\]\nThe metric is Lipschitz. Replace the time coordinate by\n\\[\n\\widetilde t(t)=t-\\epsilon_1(e^{H(t_f-t)}-1).\n\\]\nIf \\(\\Delta=t-\\widetilde t\\) and\n\\[\n\\eta=\\widetilde t'-1=\\epsilon_1He^{H(t_f-t)},\n\\]\nthen \\(0\\le\\Delta\\le\\eta/H\\). Metric displacement changes the quadratic form by at most \\(C\\Delta(1+\\eta)^2\\), while adding \\(\\eta\\partial_t\\) contributes at most \\(-2c_0\\eta\\), with nonpositive quadratic contribution. Choose \\(H\\) large and then \\(\\epsilon_1\\) small so that \\(\\eta\\le1\\). The modified curve is uniformly future timelike, has the same final point, and starts in \\(I^+(\\Sigma)\\).</p><p>A sufficiently fine polygonal interpolation remains uniformly future timelike: on each short interval average velocities in a bounded convex timelike cone for a frozen metric, and use continuity to retain a smaller margin. Follow the polygon to its first exit \\(z\\). The straight piece immediately before \\(z\\) has constant spatial slope \\(v_*\\), stays interior before \\(z\\), and is uniformly timelike up to \\(z\\).</p><p>Take \\(l&gt;0\\) small, \\(t_0=t(z)-l\\), \\(t_1=t(z)+l\\). Start an open six-dimensional family of intrinsic unit timelike geodesics at \\(t_0\\), with position within \\(l^2\\) of the straight piece and slope within \\(l\\) of \\(v_*\\). Bounded ambient Christoffel symbols and the equations in Lemma 2.4 imply\n\\[\n|V^i-v_*^i|\\le(1+2C_0)l,\\qquad\n|x(t)-x_*(t)|\\le C_1l^2\n\\]\non chart-time intervals of length \\(2l\\). A first-exit argument keeps them in a fixed box and in one uniformly timelike slope neighborhood. Unit normalization gives uniform positive upper and lower bounds for \\(dt/d\\tau\\).</p><p>If one geodesic remained in \\(\\mathcal M_d\\) through \\(t_1\\), add a spatial bump supported in \\((t_0,t_1)\\), of height \\(O(l^2)\\) and derivative \\(O(l)\\), moving its position at \\(t(z)\\) to \\(z\\). Scaling the bump gives a fixed-endpoint timelike homotopy. Lemma 2.3 would force the final path to remain in \\(\\mathcal M_d\\), although it passes through \\(z\\notin\\mathcal M_d\\). Thus all members have maximal intrinsic chart-time at most \\(t_1\\). The bounds give finite remaining proper time and ambient limits of position and tangent. Their position limits are outside \\(\\mathcal M_d\\), or intrinsic smooth continuation would be possible. These are endpoints in the full development, not merely in an auxiliary region. No ambient geodesic is solved beyond \\(\\mathcal M_d\\).</p><p>Continue one starting state backwards. Lemma 2.4 gives a transverse crossing of \\(\\Sigma\\). Smooth geodesic flow maps an open neighborhood in \\(T\\Sigma\\) diffeomorphically to part of the starting-state family. Shrink to a relatively compact neighborhood with closure mapped into that family. Its earlier geodesic tube is compact and has uniformly bounded proper duration; so does the final chart part. Hence \\(T_d(w)\\le B_0\\) uniformly there.</p><p>Initial \\(k_{d,w}\\)-orthonormal frames and inverse matrices are bounded on the seed closure. Parallel transport on the compact earlier tube and then through the final bounded chart-time interval preserves uniform bounds. A finite earlier chart cover with smaller cores, together with the final ambient chart, gives one positive chart margin. Lemma 2.5 supplies one \\(l_*\\) and \\(C_*\\) for every base point on every geodesic in the neighborhood, including arbitrarily late base points.</p><p>Choose\n\\[\nB\\ge\\max\\{1,B_0,C_*,l_*^{-1}\\}\n\\]\nan integer and \\(O\\in\\mathscr O\\) inside the neighborhood. The duration and transport requirements hold, proving \\(d\\in\\mathcal A(O,B)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The positive metric \\(r_U\\) is parallel along \\(\\gamma_{d,w}\\), and \\( c(w)^{-1}k_{d,w}\\le r_U\\le c(w)k_{d,w}, \\qquad c(w)=\\left(\\sqrt{1+h(w,w)}+\\sqrt{h(w,w)}\\right)^2. \\) The comparison is uniform for data and seeds in …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the seed, geodesic, and positive metric of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(U=\\dot\\gamma_{d,w}\\) and\n  \\[\n  r_U=g_d+2U^\\flat\\otimes U^\\flat.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The positive metric \\(r_U\\) is parallel along \\(\\gamma_{d,w}\\), and\n\\[\nc(w)^{-1}k_{d,w}\\le r_U\\le c(w)k_{d,w},\n\\qquad\nc(w)=\\left(\\sqrt{1+h(w,w)}+\\sqrt{h(w,w)}\\right)^2.\n\\]\nThe comparison is uniform for data and seeds in a sufficiently small neighborhood of a fixed pair.</p>", "proof": "<p>Both \\(g_d\\) and \\(U\\) are parallel. At the initial point choose an \\(h\\)-orthonormal spatial frame with first vector along \\(w\\), if \\(w\\ne0\\), and set \\(s=h(w,w)\\), \\(\\alpha=\\sqrt{1+s}\\). On the normal and first spatial directions the matrix of \\(r_U\\) relative to \\(k_{d,w}(0)\\) is\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n1+2s&amp;-2\\alpha\\sqrt s\\\\\n-2\\alpha\\sqrt s&amp;1+2s\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nIts eigenvalues are \\((\\alpha+\\sqrt s)^2\\) and \\((\\alpha-\\sqrt s)^2\\), which are reciprocal. The other two eigenvalues are one. This also covers \\(s=0\\), and parallel transport preserves the inequalities.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Each \\(\\mathcal A(O,B)\\) is relatively closed in \\(\\mathcal U\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the passing sets of Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Each \\(\\mathcal A(O,B)\\) is relatively closed in \\(\\mathcal U\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Every failure has a compact strict witness. If \\(T_d(w)&gt;B\\) for one \\(w\\in O\\), choose \\(B&lt;b&lt;T_d(w)\\); the geodesic segment through proper time \\(b\\) is such a witness. Otherwise a failure is witnessed by one seed, one positive time below its maximal duration, and one loop satisfying\n\\[\nL(\\ell)&lt;B^{-1},\n\\qquad\n\\|P_\\ell-\\operatorname{Id}\\|_{k_{d,w}(\\tau)}&gt;BL(\\ell).\n\\]</p><p>Smooth compact-subset vacuum Cauchy stability and geometric uniqueness apply. Enlarge the witness by finitely many local evolution neighborhoods and their causal shadows on \\(\\Sigma\\). These shadows are compact. Finite local Cauchy stability and uniqueness on overlaps give embeddings of a neighborhood of the witness into nearby developments with smooth metric convergence; only finitely many finite derivative orders are needed.</p><p>For a loop witness also include compact timelike arcs from \\(\\Sigma\\) to finitely many neighborhoods covering its image. Under a small data change the arcs remain timelike, and their final short segments can vary within those neighborhoods. The nearby loop consequently remains in \\(I^+(\\Sigma)\\).</p><p>The initial velocity, geodesic on the chosen compact interval, and parallel transport depend continuously on the data. Move the loop base to the nearby geodesic point by a local diffeomorphism converging smoothly to the identity. Its developed length and transport matrix converge, so both strict inequalities persist. The duration witness also persists beyond \\(B\\). Thus the complement is open. No continuity of \\(T_d(w)\\) at a maximal endpoint is asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every path \\(H(\\lambda,\\cdot)\\) lies in \\(P\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P\\) be a smooth globally hyperbolic open subspacetime of a Hausdorff manifold \\(\\widetilde M\\) carrying a continuous Lorentzian metric.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H:[0,1]^2\\to\\widetilde M\\) be continuous, with fixed endpoints \\(H(\\lambda,0)=p\\), \\(H(\\lambda,1)=q\\), where \\(p,q\\in P\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Every \\(H(\\lambda,\\cdot)\\) is future causal, and \\(H(0,\\cdot)\\) lies in \\(P\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every path \\(H(\\lambda,\\cdot)\\) lies in \\(P\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(E\\) be the parameters whose whole paths lie in \\(P\\). Compactness of the parameter interval of each path and openness of \\(P\\) make \\(E\\) open. Every such path lies in the intrinsic compact diamond\n\\[\nD=J_P^+(p)\\cap J_P^-(q).\n\\]\nIts image in the Hausdorff ambient manifold is closed. If \\(\\lambda_j\\in E\\) and \\(\\lambda_j\\to\\lambda\\), continuity puts every \\(H(\\lambda,s)\\) in that closed image, so \\(E\\) is closed. It is nonempty and therefore all of \\([0,1]\\). No ambient causal convexity or ambient geodesic uniqueness is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "Lemma 3.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The geodesic has no endpoint in the spacetime as an unparametrized causal curve.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Consider a maximal timelike geodesic in a smooth spacetime.</p><p class=\"li\">For the final assertion, \\(\\Sigma\\) is a Cauchy hypersurface and the geodesic passes through a point of \\(I^+(\\Sigma)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The geodesic has no endpoint in the spacetime as an unparametrized causal curve. Its maximal past continuation from the stated point crosses \\(\\Sigma\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose a unit geodesic has an interior position limit. In a precompact temporal chart \\((t,x)\\) about the limit, causal slopes \\(V=(1,dx/dt)\\) are uniformly bounded on a tail. With \\(\\dot t=dt/d\\tau&gt;0\\), the geodesic equation reads\n\\[\n\\frac d{dt}\\log\\dot t=-\\Gamma^0_{\\alpha\\beta}V^\\alpha V^\\beta,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{dV^i}{dt}\n=-\\Gamma^i_{\\alpha\\beta}V^\\alpha V^\\beta\n+\\Gamma^0_{\\alpha\\beta}V^\\alpha V^\\beta V^i.\n\\]\nThe right sides are bounded on the finite chart-time tail. Hence \\(\\dot t\\) is bounded above and below by positive constants and has a limit, as does \\(V\\). The remaining proper time is finite, and the limiting unit tangent and position give smooth ODE continuation, contradicting maximality. The past case is identical.</p><p>Complete the maximal past geodesic by any future-inextendible timelike continuation. Cauchyness gives a crossing of \\(\\Sigma\\), and achronality places it before the given point of \\(I^+(\\Sigma)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.5", "orig": "Lemma 3.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are \\(l_*>0\\) and \\(C_*<\\infty\\), depending only on these constants, such that every piecewise smooth loop of developed length \\(L<l_*\\) stays in its selected chart and satisfies …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Consider base points in regular coordinate charts for a \\(C^1\\) Lorentzian metric.</p><p class=\"li\">Christoffel symbols are uniformly bounded; every base point has coordinate distance at least \\(\\rho&gt;0\\) from its selected chart boundary.</p><p class=\"li\">The coordinate matrices of the prescribed positive orthonormal frames and their inverses have norm at most \\(K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Developed length and based transport are defined as in Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(l_*&gt;0\\) and \\(C_*&lt;\\infty\\), depending only on these constants, such that every piecewise smooth loop of developed length \\(L&lt;l_*\\) stays in its selected chart and satisfies\n\\[\n\\|P_\\ell-\\operatorname{Id}\\|_k\\le C_*L.\n\\]\nThe loop may initially be specified in any open subspacetime of the ambient chart; coordinate smallness is not assumed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Continue the initial frame \\(F_0\\) parallel along the loop and write \\(\\dot\\ell=Fz\\). Until the first chart departure,\n\\[\n|\\dot F|\\le C\\|F\\|^2|z|,\n\\qquad\n|\\dot\\ell|\\le\\|F\\||z|.\n\\]\nUsing accumulated developed length \\(s=\\int|z|\\), comparison with \\(f'=Cf^2\\), \\(f(0)=K\\), gives\n\\[\n\\|F\\|\\le\\frac K{1-CKs}\\le2K\n\\]\nfor \\(s\\le(2CK)^{-1}\\). Thus displacement is at most \\(2Ks\\). Choose\n\\[\nl_*&lt;\\min\\{(2CK)^{-1},\\rho/(4K)\\}.\n\\]\nA first exit before length \\(l_*\\) would require displacement at least \\(\\rho\\) and at most \\(\\rho/2\\), a contradiction.</p><p>The inequalities hold on the entire loop and yield\n\\[\n\\|F(1)-F_0\\|\\le4CK^2L.\n\\]\nBecause the loop returns to its base, its matrix is \\(F_0^{-1}F(1)\\); multiplication by the bounded inverse gives the assertion, for example with \\(C_*=4CK^3\\). Piecewise smooth parametrizations cause no change in the integral estimates.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Corollary 3.11", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every \\(d\\in\\mathcal U\\) has the comparison development described in Lemma 3.1 below.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the normalized Kerr center \\(0&lt;a&lt;1\\), and reduce the defining \\(p_{10}\\)-radius of \\(\\mathcal U\\) as required by [20].</p><p class=\"li\">Use the passing tests and seeds of Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every \\(d\\in\\mathcal U\\) has the comparison development described in Lemma 3.1 below. If \\(d\\in\\mathcal A(O,B)\\), there is \\(w\\in O\\) whose first runout from that comparison development is a noncorner branch.</p><p>After exchanging the optical labels and preparing the angular coordinates as in [20, Lemma 7.8], the branch is\n\\[\nu\\to u_*&lt;\\infty,\\qquad v\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nThe exchange changes neither the geodesic nor its initial seed.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(d\\in\\mathcal A(O,B)\\), every \\(w\\in O\\) has finite full-development duration. Lemma 3.1 therefore classifies every first runout. The nonempty open set \\(O\\) has positive canonical seed measure and cannot consist entirely of the null set in Lemma 3.3. Choose a noncorner seed.</p><p>The exchange lemma [20, Lemma 7.8] changes the coordinate preparation on the same cylinder and product spacetime and retains the estimates. It does not change the observer or seed. No curvature bound or regular ambient endpoint has been imposed on the selected observer.</p><p>Consequently, to destroy each closed test it remains to construct, for every passing \\(d\\), finite \\(m\\ge10\\), and \\(r&gt;0\\), a datum \\(d'\\in\\mathcal U\\) with\n\\[\np_m(d'-d)&lt;r,\\qquad d'\\notin\\mathcal A(O,B).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Proposition 3.8", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a smooth globally hyperbolic vacuum comparison development \\(\\mathcal P_d\\), with the entire bridge \\(\\Sigma\\) Cauchy, openly and isometrically embedded in \\(\\mathcal M_d\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(d\\in\\mathcal U\\) at a fixed rotating subextremal center.</p><p class=\"li\">The geometric inputs are [20, Proposition 8.2, Proposition 8.5, and Corollary 8.6], with smooth complete signed two-ended vacuum data, finite weighted symbol seminorms, and only \\(p_{10}\\) small.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a smooth globally hyperbolic vacuum comparison development \\(\\mathcal P_d\\), with the entire bridge \\(\\Sigma\\) Cauchy, openly and isometrically embedded in \\(\\mathcal M_d\\). It contains a spacelike cylinder \\(S\\) and its full future product wedge\n\\[\n[0,\\infty)_t\\times\\mathbb R_x\\times\\mathbb S^2,\\qquad\nt=(u+v)/2,\\quad x=(v-u)/2,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\ng=-2a\\,du\\,dv+\\gamma_{AB}(d\\theta^A-b^Adu)(d\\theta^B-b^Bdu),\n\\qquad\na=qA,\\quad q=q_0e^{-\\kappa(u+v)}.\n\\]\nHere \\(q_0,\\kappa&gt;0\\); \\(A^{\\pm1}\\) and \\(\\gamma^{\\pm1}\\) are uniformly bounded, and \\(\\gamma\\) is uniformly comparable with a fixed round metric.</p><p>Every seed with \\(T_d(w)&lt;\\infty\\) crosses \\(S\\) before first runout from \\(\\mathcal P_d\\), and that runout has exactly one of the limits\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{ll}\nu\\to u_*&lt;\\infty,\\quad v\\to\\infty,&amp;\\text{first branch},\\\\\nv\\to v_*&lt;\\infty,\\quad u\\to\\infty,&amp;\\text{second branch},\\\\\nu\\to\\infty,\\quad v\\to\\infty,&amp;\\text{corner}.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThese are coordinate regimes; no boundary points are adjoined and \\(\\mathcal P_d\\) is not identified with all of \\(\\mathcal M_d\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The cited comparison construction uses the finite bridge evolution, two exterior evolutions, and full-cylinder interior continuation. Its weighted powers and seminorms agree with the present phase space; equivalent reference atlases are accommodated by reducing the radius. It requires no fixed charges, curvature bound, or extension.</p><p>Let \\(\\tau_{\\mathcal P}\\) be the supremum of times for which the initial connected geodesic segment stays in \\(\\mathcal P_d\\). Then\n\\[\n\\tau_{\\mathcal P}\\le T_d(w)&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nThis is a maximal future geodesic segment in \\(\\mathcal P_d\\). The cited exterior completeness statement forces it to cross \\(S\\). Both \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) strictly increase in the wedge. If both had finite limits, the position would remain in a compact regular coordinate range, and Lemma 2.4 would give continuation. Hence one or both diverge, giving the three alternatives.</p><p>If \\(\\tau_{\\mathcal P}&lt;T_d(w)\\), its endpoint is an ordinary point of \\(\\mathcal M_d\\), though not a point of this wedge; the classification is unchanged.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.9", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Along the remaining wedge track, each \\(|\\dot Y_i|\\) has positive upper and lower bounds and \\(|\\Xi|_\\gamma\\) is bounded.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In the wedge of Lemma 3.1, put \\(y_0=u\\), \\(y_1=v\\),\n  \\[\n  L_0=\\partial_u+b^A\\partial_A,\\qquad L_1=\\partial_v,\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  \\chi_i=\\tfrac12\\mathcal L_{L_i}\\gamma,\\qquad\n  \\ell_i=L_i\\log a.\n  \\]\n  Define \\(\\xi_i\\) by\n  \\[\n  \\xi_0+\\xi_1=d_\\theta\\log a,\\qquad\n  \\xi_0-\\xi_1=-a^{-1}\\gamma(\\partial_vb,\\cdot).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The low estimates imported from [20, Equations (8.22)–(8.23)] are\n  \\[\n  |\\xi_i|_\\gamma\\le C,\\qquad\n  |\\chi_i|_\\gamma+|\\ell_i+\\kappa|\n  \\le C(q+g_i(y_i)),\\qquad\n  |b|_{\\mathrm{round}}\\le C(q+g_0(u)),\n  \\]\n  with nonnegative \\(g_i\\in L^1(\\mathbb R)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let a future unit timelike geodesic begin at a fixed wedge point. Set\n  \\[\n  Y_i=e^{-\\kappa y_i},\\qquad\n  \\Xi=\\dot\\theta-b\\dot u.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Along the remaining wedge track, each \\(|\\dot Y_i|\\) has positive upper and lower bounds and \\(|\\Xi|_\\gamma\\) is bounded. Constants may depend on the initial unit tangent. In particular each corner track has one finite bound for both \\(Y\\)-speeds and \\(\\Xi\\) on its entire sufficiently late tail.</p>", "proof": "<p>Koszul’s formula gives, for angular \\(E\\) and \\(j\\ne i\\),\n\\[\n\\nabla_{L_i}L_i=\\ell_iL_i,\\qquad\n\\nabla_E L_i=\\chi_i(E)^\\sharp+\\xi_i(E)L_i,\\qquad\n\\nabla_{L_j}L_i=a\\xi_j^\\sharp.\n\\]\nWrite\n\\[\nU=\\dot y_0L_0+\\dot y_1L_1+\\Xi,\\qquad\np_i=-g(U,L_i)=a\\dot y_j&gt;0.\n\\]\nUnit normalization and differentiation of \\(p_i\\) give\n\\[\n1+|\\Xi|_\\gamma^2=2a\\dot y_0\\dot y_1,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{\\dot p_i}{p_i}\n=\\ell_i\\dot y_i+(\\xi_i-\\xi_j)(\\Xi)\n-\\frac{\\chi_i(\\Xi,\\Xi)}{p_i}.\n\\]\nSince \\(|\\Xi|_\\gamma^2/p_i\\le2\\dot y_i\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac d{d\\tau}\\log(e^{\\kappa y_i}p_i)\\right|\n\\le C(q+g_i(y_i))\\dot y_i+C|\\Xi|_\\gamma,\n\\qquad\ne^{\\kappa y_i}p_i=\\frac{q_0}{\\kappa}A|\\dot Y_j|.\n\\]</p><p>The positive \\(Y_i\\) decrease. On any initial portion of the remaining track,\n\\[\n\\int|\\Xi|_\\gamma\\,d\\tau\n\\le C\\left(\\int|\\dot Y_0|\\,d\\tau\n\\int|\\dot Y_1|\\,d\\tau\\right)^{1/2}\n\\le C\\sqrt{Y_0^{\\mathrm{in}}Y_1^{\\mathrm{in}}},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\int q\\dot y_i\\,d\\tau\\le\\frac{q_0}{\\kappa}Y_0^{\\mathrm{in}}Y_1^{\\mathrm{in}},\n\\qquad\n\\int g_i(y_i)\\dot y_i\\,d\\tau\n\\le\\int_{y_i^{\\mathrm{in}}}^\\infty g_i(s)\\,ds.\n\\]\nThe logarithmic variation is therefore bounded independently of the terminal point. Bounds on \\(A^{\\pm1}\\) give the claimed bounds on both \\(|\\dot Y_i|\\). Finally,\n\\[\n1+|\\Xi|_\\gamma^2\n=\\frac{2q_0A}{\\kappa^2}|\\dot Y_0||\\dot Y_1|\n\\]\nbounds \\(\\Xi\\). No uniform bound over all unit timelike tangents is asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "Proposition 3.10", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Seeds whose first runout from \\(\\mathcal P_d\\) is a corner lie in a set of measure zero for every smooth positive measure on \\(T\\Sigma\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(d\\in\\mathcal U\\), its comparison development, and the coordinates of Lemmas 3.1–3.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Seeds whose first runout from \\(\\mathcal P_d\\) is a corner lie in a set of measure zero for every smooth positive measure on \\(T\\Sigma\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Consider the null sections\n\\[\nN_j=\\{u=j,\\ u+v&gt;0\\},\\qquad j=1,2,\\ldots.\n\\]\nEvery corner track hits all sufficiently late sections transversely. Tangential covariant momenta are\n\\[\n\\pi_{Y_1}\n=-\\frac{a\\dot Y_0}{\\kappa^2Y_0Y_1}\n=-\\frac{q_0A}{\\kappa^2}\\dot Y_0,\n\\qquad\nP_A=\\gamma_{AB}\\Xi^B.\n\\]\nThe shift contributes only to the other radial covector component. Lemma 3.2 gives one eventual finite momentum bound along each corner track, and \\(Y_1\\to0\\).</p><p>On \\(T^*\\mathcal P_d\\), use\n\\[\n\\omega=d\\pi_\\alpha\\wedge dx^\\alpha,\\qquad\n\\mathcal H=\\tfrac12g^{-1}(\\pi,\\pi),\n\\]\nand the future unit shell \\(\\mathcal H=-1/2\\). Above \\(N_j\\), the six variables\n\\[\n(Y_1,\\theta^1,\\theta^2,\\pi_{Y_1},P_1,P_2)\n\\]\nare coordinates on the shell section. Indeed, with\n\\[\n\\widetilde a=\\frac a{\\kappa^2Y_0Y_1}&gt;0,\\qquad\n\\widetilde b=-\\frac b{\\kappa Y_0},\n\\]\nthe shell equation is\n\\[\n-\\widetilde a^{-1}\n(\\pi_{Y_0}+\\widetilde b^AP_A)\\pi_{Y_1}\n+\\tfrac12\\gamma^{AB}P_AP_B=-\\tfrac12.\n\\]\nFuture timelikeness implies \\(\\dot Y_0&lt;0\\), hence \\(\\pi_{Y_1}&gt;0\\), so \\(\\pi_{Y_0}\\) is uniquely determined. Restricting \\(\\omega\\) gives\n\\[\n\\omega_j=d\\pi_{Y_1}\\wedge dY_1+\\sum_{A=1}^2dP_A\\wedge d\\theta^A.\n\\]\nIts absolute symplectic volume is ordinary canonical coordinate volume, without a lapse or \\(Y_0\\) factor.</p><p>Let \\(D_j\\subset T\\Sigma\\) be the seeds whose initial connected comparison track hits \\(N_j\\) at finite time. Transversality and smooth flow make \\(D_j\\) open and the hitting map \\(F_j\\) smooth. It is injective: a wedge trajectory hits \\(N_j\\) once, and a maximal trajectory in the globally hyperbolic comparison development meets its Cauchy surface once.</p><p>The tangential initial covector is \\(h(w,\\cdot)\\), a smooth fiberwise isomorphism from \\(T\\Sigma\\) to \\(T^*\\Sigma\\). Pull back canonical volume to obtain a smooth positive measure \\(\\mu\\) on seeds. Hamiltonian flow preserves section symplectic forms even with variable hitting time:\n\\[\ndF_j(V)=d\\Phi_{\\tau(w)}(V)+X_{\\mathcal H}(F_j(w))\\,d\\tau(V).\n\\]\nThe \\(d\\tau\\)-terms vanish against shell-tangent vectors because \\(X_{\\mathcal H}\\) lies in the kernel of the restricted \\(\\omega\\). Nondegeneracy also makes \\(F_j\\) a local diffeomorphism; injectivity makes it a diffeomorphism onto its open image.</p><p>For fixed integer \\(N\\ge1\\), the target region\n\\[\n0&lt;Y_1&lt;r,\\qquad |\\pi_{Y_1}|\\le N,\\qquad |P|_{\\mathrm{round}}\\le N\n\\]\nhas canonical volume at most \\(C_Nr\\), uniformly in \\(j\\), by a finite angular chart cover. Its inverse image \\(S_{j,r,N}\\) therefore satisfies\n\\[\n\\mu(S_{j,r,N})\\le C_Nr.\n\\]\nFatou gives\n\\[\n\\mu(\\liminf_{j\\to\\infty}S_{j,r,N})\\le C_Nr;\n\\]\nfiniteness of \\(\\mu(T\\Sigma)\\) is unnecessary.</p><p>Every corner seed lies in\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{N=1}^\\infty\\bigcap_{s=1}^\\infty\n\\liminf_{j\\to\\infty}S_{j,1/s,N}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(N\\) above that track’s eventual momentum bound; every sufficiently late hit then lies in each prescribed \\(Y_1&lt;1/s\\) strip. For fixed \\(N\\), the intersection has measure at most \\(C_N/s\\) for every \\(s\\), hence is null. The countable union is null. All smooth positive seed measures have the same null sets.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Proposition 4.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a defining \\(p_{10}\\)-smallness threshold, depending only on the fixed center and preparation, for which:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the normalized rotating subextremal center, a smooth datum \\(d\\), and the comparison coordinates of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  t=(u+v)/2,\\quad x=(v-u)/2,\\quad S=\\{t=0\\},\\quad\n  a=qA,\\quad q=q_0e^{-2\\kappa t}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">In a fixed finite sphere atlas, set \\(y_0=u,y_1=v,B_0=b,B_1=0\\), and\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_i=\\partial_{y_i}+\\mathcal L_{B_i},\\quad\n  \\chi_i=\\tfrac12\\mathcal L_i\\gamma,\\quad\n  \\tau_i=\\operatorname{tr}_\\gamma\\chi_i,\\quad\n  \\ell_i=L_i\\log a,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\xi_0+\\xi_1=d_\\theta\\log a,\\qquad\n  \\xi_0-\\xi_1=-a^{-1}\\gamma(\\partial_vb,\\cdot),\\qquad\n  \\beta_i=\\operatorname{div}_\\gamma\\chi_i-d_\\theta\\tau_i.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Ordinary derivatives \\(D\\) mean coordinate derivatives of these arrays.</p><p class=\"li\">The notation \\(Q_T\\le e^{o(T)}\\) means that for every \\(\\sigma&gt;0\\), \\(Q_T\\le C_\\sigma e^{\\sigma T}\\) for sufficiently large \\(T\\). Constants may depend on the fixed datum, domains, and every fixed derivative and formal order. Two-sided subexponential size means the same for a positive quantity and its reciprocal. No uniformity over unbounded derivative orders is intended.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a defining \\(p_{10}\\)-smallness threshold, depending only on the fixed center and preparation, for which:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. The cylinder develops on the full wedge. The arrays \\(A^{\\pm1},\\gamma^{\\pm1}\\) are uniformly bounded in angular \\(H^6\\), and \\(\\xi_i,a^{-1}\\partial_vb\\) in angular \\(H^5\\). Nonnegative envelopes \\(g_i(y_i)\\), with summable unit-cell suprema, satisfy\n   \\[\n   \\|\\chi_i,\\ell_i+\\kappa\\|_{H^4}\\le C(q+g_i(y_i)),\n   \\qquad\n   \\|b\\|_{H^5}\\le C(q+g_0(u)).\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The quantities\n   \\[\n   \\|b\\|_{H^5}+\\sum_i\\|\\chi_i,\\ell_i+\\kappa\\|_{H^4},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=0}^1\\left(\n   \\|\\partial_{y_k}b\\|_{H^2}\n   +\\sum_i\\|\\partial_{y_k}\\chi_i,\\partial_{y_k}\\ell_i\\|_{H^2}\n   \\right),\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=0}^1\n   \\left(\\|\\partial_{y_k}\\gamma\\|_{H^4}\n   +\\|\\partial_{y_k}\\log a+\\kappa\\|_{H^4}\\right)\n   \\]\n   are uniformly bounded and tend to zero when \\(t,|u|,|v|\\to\\infty\\). In particular \\(D\\log a,D\\gamma,b,Db\\) are bounded.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For fixed \\(U\\in\\mathbb R\\), \\(C&gt;0\\), and any fixed finite order, all ordinary derivatives of\n   \\[\n   \\gamma^{\\pm1},\\quad\\log A,\\quad b,\\quad a^{-1}\\partial_vb\n   \\]\n   are \\(e^{o(T)}\\) on\n   \\[\n   t\\ge0,\\qquad y_d\\le U,\\qquad y_l\\le CT,\\qquad\n   \\{d,l\\}=\\{0,1\\}.\n   \\]\n   This includes restrictions and coordinate-path subsegments. Derivatives of \\(a,\\sqrt a,\\partial_vb\\) retain factors \\(a,\\sqrt a,a\\), respectively.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Either branch can be independently prepared in the convention \\(v\\to\\infty\\), \\(u\\to u_*&lt;\\infty\\), retaining all conclusions on the same product spacetime.</p><p>No higher seminorm, radiation coefficient, or observer-measured curvature is required small or bounded.</p>", "proof": "<p>The exterior input is simultaneous closure [20, Proposition 6.4] and the higher estimates [20, Proposition 5.6], with variable asymptotic parameters and arbitrary finite higher symbol seminorms.</p><p>For nearby physical Kerr parameters, [20, Lemma 7.1] supplies exact profiles smooth in \\((q,\\theta)\\) and the parameters, with \\(A,\\gamma\\) and their inverses smooth at \\(q=0\\), and\n\\[\nb=O(q),\\qquad\n\\chi_i,\\ell_i+\\kappa,\\beta_i=O(q),\\qquad\n\\xi_i,K_\\gamma,\\operatorname{curl}_\\gamma\\xi_i=O(1)\n\\]\nafter each fixed angular or parameter derivative. Rescaling exact optical variables by \\(\\kappa_-/\\kappa\\) gives one common \\(\\kappa\\).</p><p>The moving preparation uses integrated Killing and rotation phases with derivatives equal to the instantaneous frozen frequencies. By [20, Lemma 7.2], Jacobian errors contain positive parameter derivatives with bounded coefficients and no elapsed-time factor. The physical profile depends only on \\(u\\); frozen differentiation means differentiation at fixed parameters followed by evaluation at that profile, so the parameter defect is confined to \\(u\\).</p><p>After reducing the neighborhood, the cylinder differences from the frozen profiles have arbitrarily small sums of unit-\\(x\\)-cell suprema in\n\\[\nA,\\log A,\\gamma,b,\\chi_i,\\ell_i,\\xi_i:\\ H^6_\\theta,\\qquad\n\\gamma:\\ H^7_\\theta,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\partial_x(\\chi_i,\\ell_i,\\xi_i,b)|_S:\\ H^2_\\theta.\n\\]\nThe first profile derivative also has a small unit-cell sum, and every fixed higher entry norm on \\(|x|\\le CT\\) is subexponential. These are the hypotheses and conclusions of [20, Proposition 7.3]. Its trace argument uses mixed metric derivatives through eight and one additional time derivative outside trapping, supplied by [20, Proposition 5.3]. The entry supports are between the horizons and separated from trapping; the bounded middle uses finite bridge evolution. Thus no extra high-order smallness is needed beyond \\(p_{10}\\).</p><p>Apply [20, Propositions 7.4–7.6] to these profiles and entry norms. They give the displayed low and high estimates, including the pure longitudinal derivatives \\(L_i\\chi_i\\) and \\(L_i\\ell_i\\). Proposition 7.7 there first exhausts the wedge using finite-diamond high estimates where \\(a\\) is bounded below. Only after exhaustion are summable-envelope tails used for expanding-domain subexponential estimates. Differentiating\n\\[\na=qA,\\qquad\n\\partial_vb=a\\gamma^{-1}(\\xi_1-\\xi_0)\n\\]\ngives the factor assertions.</p><p>For the opposite branch, [20, Lemma 7.8] negates the prepared \\(x\\) on \\(S\\) and transports newly prepared angles along the new shift-free direction. At every finite point this is a finite smooth sphere flow with smooth inverse. Reapply entry and finite-diamond estimates and exhaust. No estimate for an otherwise uncontrolled long angular coordinate change is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Lemma 4.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "On a late segment, \\( p\\asymp1,\\qquad |P|+|\\Xi|+|\\dot u|\\le C. \\) The angular position has a limit, and \\( a_p:=a(p_T)\\asymp e^{-\\kappa T}, \\qquad \\dot v(p_T)\\asymp e^{\\kappa T}. \\) No curvature hypothesis is required.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the background wedge and low estimates of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Let a future unit timelike geodesic approach \\(v\\to\\infty\\), \\(u\\to u_*&lt;\\infty\\).</p><p class=\"li\">With proper time \\(\\tau\\), define\n  \\[\n  p=a\\dot v,\\qquad\n  \\Xi=\\dot\\theta-b\\dot u,\\qquad\n  P=\\gamma\\Xi.\n  \\]\n  Let \\(p_T\\) be the point where \\(v=T\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On a late segment,\n\\[\np\\asymp1,\\qquad |P|+|\\Xi|+|\\dot u|\\le C.\n\\]\nThe angular position has a limit, and\n\\[\na_p:=a(p_T)\\asymp e^{-\\kappa T},\n\\qquad\n\\dot v(p_T)\\asymp e^{\\kappa T}.\n\\]\nNo curvature hypothesis is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Unit normalization gives\n\\[\n1+|\\Xi|_\\gamma^2=2a\\dot u\\dot v=2p\\dot u.\n\\]\nDifferentiating \\(p=-g(U,L_0)\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac{\\dot p}{p}\n=\\ell_0\\dot u+(\\xi_0-\\xi_1)(\\Xi)\n-\\frac{\\chi_0(\\Xi,\\Xi)}p.\n\\]\nThe null connection identities imply\n\\[\n|\\dot{\\log p}|\\le C(\\dot u+|\\Xi|_\\gamma).\n\\]\nMoreover\n\\[\n\\int|\\Xi|_\\gamma\\,d\\tau\n\\le\\sqrt2\\left(\\int du\\right)^{1/2}\n\\left(\\int a\\,dv\\right)^{1/2}&lt;\\infty,\n\\]\nsince \\(u\\) has finite increasing variation and \\(a\\le Ce^{-\\kappa v}\\). Thus \\(p\\) has positive upper and lower bounds. Bounded \\(b\\) then gives finite angular length and an angular limit.</p><p>Near that limit use a sphere chart and canonical momenta\n\\[\n\\pi_u=-p-b\\cdot P.\n\\]\nSolving the unit shell with \\(v\\) as time gives\n\\[\n-\\pi_v=\\frac{a(1+|P|_{\\gamma^{-1}}^2)}{2p},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{du}{dv}\n=\\frac{a(1+|P|_{\\gamma^{-1}}^2)}{2p^2}.\n\\]\nAt fixed \\((\\pi_u,P)\\),\n\\[\n\\partial_\\theta p=-(\\partial_\\theta b)\\cdot P.\n\\]\nBounded first derivatives of \\(\\log a,\\gamma,b\\) in Hamilton’s equation yield\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac{dP}{dv}\\right|\n\\le C(p+|P|)\\frac{du}{dv}.\n\\]\nGronwall in the finite-variation variable \\(u\\) bounds \\(P\\), then the normalization bounds \\(\\Xi\\) and \\(\\dot u\\). The formula \\(a=qA\\) gives the final asymptotics.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 4.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The launch patch near \\(v=x=T\\) on \\(S\\) occurs at \\(t_e=t_T+O(1)\\), with \\(t_T\\asymp T\\), and a fixed enlargement lies in \\(t_e\\le t_T+C_2\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use either independently prepared end of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(t_e\\) be regular exterior time, \\(y\\) co-moving coordinates, and \\(z=y+C_e(t_e)\\) lab coordinates, with\n  \\[\n  \\sup|C_e'|\\le s_*&lt;1/10.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The far part of \\(t_e=0\\) agrees with the original bridge.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(I\\) be the preparation parameter, \\(0&lt;c_I\\le I'\\le C_I\\), and \\(I(t_T)=T\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The launch patch near \\(v=x=T\\) on \\(S\\) occurs at \\(t_e=t_T+O(1)\\), with \\(t_T\\asymp T\\), and a fixed enlargement lies in \\(t_e\\le t_T+C_2\\). Its selected-end portion \\(v&lt;(1-\\nu_0)T\\) is earlier by at least \\(c\\nu_0T-O(1)\\).</p><p>Exterior temporal levels are uniformly regular spacelike levels. First and second ordinary lab metric derivatives are bounded and become arbitrarily small at sufficiently large co-radius, uniformly in time. Every fixed mixed jet on the used \\(O(1+T)\\) ranges is \\(e^{o(T)}\\). Entry collars are noncharacteristic with controlled low and subexponential higher jets; the inner exit has a strict deeper margin.</p><p>The null covectors \\(dv\\) launched on a sufficiently small fixed entry patch escape to the past to the same original end. For every fixed \\(\\beta&gt;0\\), after choosing a sufficiently large far radius and then \\(T\\) large, arrival points satisfy\n\\[\n(1-\\beta)t_T&lt;|z-z_*|&lt;(1+\\beta)t_T,\n\\qquad z_*=C_e(t_T).\n\\]\nEntry crossing is unique and transverse and separated from other used ray portions outside a uniform collar. Every fixed Hamiltonian flow jet, including launch-center derivatives and all-subsegment propagators, is \\(e^{o(T)}\\). Tensor parallel transport and its inverse are bounded. The assertions hold on short launch enlargements through the deeper exit.</p>", "proof": "<p>Timing and low exterior estimates are supplied by [20, Sections 5, 6, and 9.4]; the escape and subsegment estimates are [20, Proposition 9.6]. The launch geometry satisfies that proposition independently of the observer.</p><p>For a nearby exact Kerr profile write its physical parameters \\(M_K,a_K\\), Boyer–Lindquist radius \\(r_K\\), and angles \\(\\omega=(\\vartheta,\\varphi)\\). Put\n\\[\n\\Delta_K=r_K^2-2M_Kr_K+a_K^2,\\qquad\nr_\\pm=M_K\\pm\\sqrt{M_K^2-a_K^2}.\n\\]\nIts optical correction satisfies\n\\[\n2(r_K^2+a_K^2)\\partial_{r_K}F_K\n+\\Delta_K(\\partial_{r_K}F_K)^2\n+|d_\\omega F_K|_{\\mathrm{rd}}^2+a_K^2\\sin^2\\vartheta=0,\n\\qquad F_K(r_-)=0.\n\\]\nThe finite root is noncharacteristic. The original depth \\(q_0\\) is chosen for both angular conventions so that\n\\[\n\\left(|\\partial_\\vartheta F_K|^2+a_K^2\\sin^2\\vartheta\\right)_{\\mathrm{entry}}\n&lt;4a_K^2.\n\\]\nThis is possible because the physical spins remain in a compact subset of \\((0,1)\\) and \\(\\partial_\\vartheta F_K=O(q_0)\\). The inner exit is fixed strictly deeper and before the inner horizon.</p><p>On fixed radial compact sets, the prepared metric and entry covectors converge in the required low orders to a nearby frozen Kerr metric and\n\\[\n\\lambda_-\\,d(r_*+F_K+T_K),\\qquad\n\\lambda_-=\\kappa_-/\\kappa&gt;0,\\qquad\n\\frac{dr_*}{dr_K}=\\frac{r_K^2+a_K^2}{\\Delta_K}.\n\\]\nIntegrated phase preparation gives this covector convergence even without convergence of accumulated azimuthal phases. The frozen axial momentum is zero, and the radial potential divided by Killing energy squared is\n\\[\n(r_K^2+a_K^2)^2\n-\\Delta_K\n\\left(|\\partial_\\vartheta F_K|^2+a_K^2\\sin^2\\vartheta\\right)_{\\mathrm{entry}}.\n\\]\nFor \\(r_K\\ge r_+\\), \\(\\Delta_K&lt;r_K^2\\) and\n\\[\n\\frac{(r_K^2+a_K^2)^2}{r_K^2}\\ge4a_K^2;\n\\]\nbetween the horizons \\(\\Delta_K&lt;0\\). The potential is strictly positive. The finite Hamilton–Jacobi root gives outward past radial direction. Thus rays cross every fixed compact band, including the event horizon in regular coordinates, transversely in bounded regular time. The entry level is strictly monotone near its crossing and separated from other compact used portions. These properties persist under the stated convergence.</p><p>In the far region, [20, Equation (9.11)] bounds the bad first-derivative contribution on dyads \\(r\\asymp R\\) by an integrated\n\\[\nO\\!\\left(R^{(e_f-(1-\\delta_f)A_H)/2}\\right),\n\\qquad e_f-(1-\\delta_f)A_H&lt;0,\n\\]\nwith the remainder summable. Hence lab velocity remains close to an outward unit direction and frequency stays nondegenerate. Integration yields the return-shell estimate, and the same connection integral bounds tensor transport and inverse.</p><p>For first Hamiltonian variation, only the momentum-to-position block can remain order one at large radius. Rescale the position variation by a small fixed constant, then take the far radius large to make the remaining blocks small using first and second metric derivatives. Compact crossing has fixed length. This gives arbitrarily small average growth on every subsegment. Higher variations have the same homogeneous propagator and lower-jet sources; induction gives subexponential jets. The argument uses launch covectors and exterior geometry, so applies at any branch foot and in the exchanged preparation.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Lemma 4.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The matrices \\(V_\\alpha\\) are bounded and, as \\(\\min(v,-u,t)\\to\\infty\\), \\( V_0\\to D_0=\\operatorname{diag}(-\\kappa/2,\\kappa/2,0,0), \\qquad V_1\\to-D_0,\\qquad V_A\\to0. \\) The differentiated connection terms contracted in …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 4 and put\n  \\[\n  \\widehat e_i=a^{-1/2}L_i,\\quad\n  \\widehat e_A=\\partial_A,\\quad\n  Z_\\alpha=\\sqrt a\\,\\widehat e_\\alpha,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  W_t=L_0+L_1,\\qquad W_x=L_1-L_0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Relative arrays of covariant two-tensors mean components in the \\(\\widehat e\\)-frame; derivatives of these arrays remain ordinary \\(D\\)-derivatives.</p><p class=\"li\">For a covariant two-tensor entry \\(n\\), let \\(n_i\\) count its slots with index \\(i\\), and put\n  \\[\n  c_n=\\frac\\kappa2(n_0-n_1),\\qquad |c_n|\\le\\kappa.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V_\\alpha\\) be the matrix of \\(\\nabla_{Z_\\alpha}\\) on the normalized vector basis.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The matrices \\(V_\\alpha\\) are bounded and, as \\(\\min(v,-u,t)\\to\\infty\\),\n\\[\nV_0\\to D_0=\\operatorname{diag}(-\\kappa/2,\\kappa/2,0,0),\n\\qquad V_1\\to-D_0,\\qquad V_A\\to0.\n\\]\nThe differentiated connection terms contracted in \\(a\\Box_g\\), and every normalized entry of \\(a\\operatorname{Riem}(g)\\), are bounded and tend to zero in this limit. Their fixed ordinary jets and bracket jets are \\(e^{o(T)}\\) on the expanding domains of Proposition 4.</p><p>The exact identity \\(\\nabla_{L_1}L_1=\\ell_1L_1\\) holds. The constant connection products are retained: on entry \\(n\\), the limiting radial operator is\n\\[\n-(L_0+c_n)(L_1-c_n)-(L_1-c_n)(L_0+c_n)\n\\]\n\\[\n=-L_0L_1-L_1L_0+2c_n(L_0-L_1)+2c_n^2,\n\\]\nthe radial part of \\(e^{2c_nx}a\\Box_g^{\\mathrm{scal}}e^{-2c_nx}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Koszul’s formula gives, with \\(j=1-i\\),\n\\[\n\\nabla_{L_i}\\widehat e_i=\\tfrac12\\ell_i\\widehat e_i,\\qquad\n\\nabla_{L_i}\\widehat e_j\n=-\\tfrac12\\ell_i\\widehat e_j+\\sqrt a\\,\\xi_i^Ae_A,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\nabla_{L_i}e_A\n=(\\chi_{i,A}{}^B-\\partial_AB_i^B)e_B\n+\\sqrt a\\,\\xi_{i,A}\\widehat e_i,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\nabla_{Z_A}\\widehat e_i\n=\\chi_{i,A}{}^Be_B\n+\\tfrac12\\sqrt a\\,(\\xi_i-\\xi_j)_A\\widehat e_i,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\nabla_{Z_A}e_B\n=\\sqrt a\\,\\Gamma(\\gamma)^C{}_{AB}e_C\n+\\chi_{0,AB}\\widehat e_1+\\chi_{1,AB}\\widehat e_0.\n\\]\nThese give the limiting matrices and \\(\\nabla_{L_i}L_i=\\ell_iL_i\\). The brackets are\n\\[\n[L_0,L_1]=\\sqrt a\\,(\\xi_0-\\xi_1)^AZ_A,\n\\]\n\\[\n[L_i,Z_A]=\\tfrac12\\ell_iZ_A-(\\partial_AB_i^B)Z_B,\n\\]\n\\[\n[Z_A,Z_B]\n=\\tfrac12\\sqrt a\\bigl((\\partial_A\\log a)Z_B-(\\partial_B\\log a)Z_A\\bigr).\n\\]</p><p>The radial trace of differentiated connection coefficients uses cross derivatives \\(L_0V_1,L_1V_0\\). In particular \\(L_1\\partial_Ab=\\partial_A\\partial_vb\\) retains \\(a\\). The vacuum torsion identities\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_i\\xi_j=\\beta_i+\\tau_i\\xi_i,\\qquad\n\\mathcal L_i\\xi_i=d_\\theta\\ell_i-\\beta_i-\\tau_i\\xi_i\n\\]\ncontrol longitudinal torsion derivatives; other angular derivatives carry \\(\\sqrt a\\). Proposition 4 therefore gives boundedness and bulk smallness of the contracted differentiated terms.</p><p>The exact curvature-matrix identity is\n\\[\naR(\\widehat e_\\alpha,\\widehat e_\\beta)\n=Z_\\alpha V_\\beta-Z_\\beta V_\\alpha\n+[V_\\alpha,V_\\beta]-V_{[Z_\\alpha,Z_\\beta]}.\n\\]\nRadial-angular terms also contain the controlled pure \\(L_i\\chi_i\\). Two angular directions carry a \\(\\sqrt a\\)-derivative or products tending to zero. The only nonzero limiting bracket coefficient multiplies \\(V_A\\to0\\), while \\([D_0,-D_0]=0\\). Angular \\(H^2\\) embeds in \\(C^0\\). Differentiating the identities gives the fixed higher-jet estimates.</p><p>The covariant tensor connection is the negative vector connection on each slot. Its constant radial actions are \\(L_0+c_n\\), \\(L_1-c_n\\). Since \\(L_0x=-1/2\\), \\(L_1x=1/2\\), expansion gives the stated conjugation and constant \\(2c_n^2\\), which is not treated as small.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 4.6 and Corollary 4.8", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Sufficiently high finite transport and Taylor orders produce real tensors \\(h_T^p,h_T^B\\) with \\( h_T^p =A_T\\operatorname{tr.rev.}_g \\operatorname{Re}\\left( e^{i\\lambda_Tv}\\sum_{j=0}^J\\lambda_T^{-j}p_j \\right). \\) Every …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a branch point \\(p_T\\) from Proposition 5 and the finite domain \\(\\mathcal D_T\\) specified in Theorem 2, first constructing on a slight enlargement.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(u_f&gt;u_2\\); the strip \\(-v\\le u\\le u_f\\), \\(v\\) near \\(T\\), lies in the expanding domains of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">For a symmetric tensor,\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{tr.rev.}_g h=h-\\tfrac12(\\operatorname{tr}_gh)g.\n  \\]\n  Let \\(\\mathcal P\\) be the trace-reversed linearized wave-map reduced vacuum operator with principal part \\(\\Box_g\\), and let \\(\\mathcal C=\\operatorname{div}_g\\) on trace-reversed tensors. On the Ricci-flat background,\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal C\\mathcal P=\\mathcal P_1\\mathcal C.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The nonlinear reduction has fixed target \\(g\\):\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Ric}(\\widetilde g)_{\\mu\\nu}\n  +\\nabla^{\\widetilde g}_{(\\mu}\\Upsilon_{\\nu)}=0,\n  \\qquad\n  \\Upsilon_\\mu\n  =-\\widetilde g_{\\mu\\lambda}\\widetilde g^{\\alpha\\beta}\n  \\bigl(\\Gamma(\\widetilde g)^\\lambda_{\\alpha\\beta}\n  -\\Gamma(g)^\\lambda_{\\alpha\\beta}\\bigr).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a finite list of output derivative orders, including an exterior base buffer \\(N_b\\). Fix \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\), put \\(\\lambda_T=e^{\\zeta T}\\), and prescribe an inverse accuracy \\(M_1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">First take \\(A_T=e^{-\\alpha T}\\), \\(\\alpha=7\\kappa/8\\). The general-amplitude conclusion below permits any fixed \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Sufficiently high finite transport and Taylor orders produce real tensors \\(h_T^p,h_T^B\\) with\n\\[\nh_T^p\n=A_T\\operatorname{tr.rev.}_g\n\\operatorname{Re}\\left(\ne^{i\\lambda_Tv}\\sum_{j=0}^J\\lambda_T^{-j}p_j\n\\right).\n\\]\nEvery fixed normalized coefficient jet of \\(p_j\\) is \\(e^{o(T)}\\). The packet is supported in a fixed-width neighborhood of \\(v=T\\) and the transported angular launch patch. Its relative array \\(q_T\\) obeys\n\\[\n\\|q_T\\|_{C^N(\\mathcal D_T)}\n\\le A_T\\lambda_T^Ne^{o(T)}\n\\]\nat each specified order.</p><p>The tensor \\(p_0\\) is trace-free and \\(p_0(L_0,\\cdot)=0\\). There is a purely angular \\(\\gamma\\)-unit vector \\(X\\) at \\(p_T\\) for which\n\\[\n\\sigma_T=\n\\left|\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(e^{i\\lambda_TT}p_0(X,X)\\bigr)\\right|\n\\]\nhas two-sided subexponential size. The \\(a\\)-scaled wave residual and \\(\\sqrt a\\)-scaled gauge residual are bounded by\n\\[\nA_T\\lambda_T^{-M_1}e^{o(T)}\n\\]\nin normalized slots at all prescribed orders.</p><p>In regular exterior lab components,\n\\[\n\\|h_T^B\\|_{C^{N_b}}\n\\le A_T\\lambda_T^{N_b+3/2}e^{o(T)}.\n\\]\nIts linear wave and gauge residuals, and its difference from \\(h_T^p\\) on the entry collar, are \\(A_T\\lambda_T^{-M_1}e^{o(T)}\\) at all prescribed finite orders, including normal spacetime jets. On the original bridge its support lies in the return shell of Proposition 6, with arbitrarily thin tube margins.</p><p>Increasing inverse accuracy and finite formal degrees does not increase these positive frequency powers.</p><p>All linear conclusions hold with any fixed amplitude \\(A_T=e^{-\\alpha T}\\), \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\), with the same order of choices. No bounded-curvature hypothesis on the branch observer is used.</p>", "proof": "<p>Set\n\\[\nk_v=g^{-1}dv=-a^{-1}L_0,\\qquad\nK_vp=p(k_v,\\cdot),\\qquad\n\\mathcal T=2\\nabla_{k_v}+\\Box_gv.\n\\]\nBecause \\(v\\) is optical, the constrained transports are\n\\[\ni\\mathcal Tp_j+\\mathcal Pp_{j-1}=0,\\qquad\niK_vp_j+\\mathcal Cp_{j-1}=0,\\qquad p_{-1}=0.\n\\]\nMultiplying transport by \\(-a/2\\) gives \\(\\nabla_{L_0}+\\tau_0/2\\). On normalized entry \\(n\\), its constant same-weight part is \\(L_0+c_n\\); other same-weight coefficients have arbitrarily small bulk average, and off-weight transport couples only from higher weights.</p><p>At \\(u=-v\\), prescribe slots with a \\(0\\)-index by the gauge equation. Free angular entries vanish for \\(j&gt;0\\); at \\(j=0\\) use compact angular and \\(v\\)-bumps times a trace-free polarization of unit size at the foot of the ray through \\(p_T\\). Negative-weight slots have zero terminal values at \\(u_f\\) and are integrated backwards. The constants \\(c_n\\) have damping sign in these directions, so a decreasing-weight triangular system determines the coefficients.</p><p>Conjugate \\(\\mathcal C\\mathcal P=\\mathcal P_1\\mathcal C\\) by the phase and compare powers. Once earlier gauge defects vanish, the next satisfies homogeneous one-form transport with zero initial value. Thus all gauge equations hold. Terminal choices impose no additional gauge condition because contraction by \\(k_v\\) uses precisely the \\(0\\)-slot entries.</p><p>Along an \\(L_0\\)-transport, the portion outside \\(\\min(v,-u,t)&gt;R\\) has length bounded in terms of \\(R,u_f\\). On the complement, \\(b\\), its first angular derivatives, and nonconstant diagonal coefficients are arbitrarily small, so their integrals are \\(o(T)\\). The angular flow and inverse have subexponential first jets. Higher variations have the same homogeneous equation and lower-jet sources with Proposition 4 coefficient bounds; induction includes the moving foot. Damping gives the same conclusion for tensor transports. Proposition 7 bounds the sources \\(a\\mathcal Pp_{j-1}\\) and \\(\\sqrt a\\,\\mathcal Cp_{j-1}\\), and integration over \\(O(T)\\) preserves subexponential bounds. The equations are local in \\(v\\) and along the angular flow, so preserve support.</p><p>The leading tensor remains trace-free and annihilates \\(k_v\\). Its screen class transports by a screen isometry times an area factor with logarithmic integral \\(o(T)\\), hence has two-sided subexponential norm. A trace-free symmetric form on a two-dimensional positive screen has a unit eigenvector with diagonal value comparable with its norm. Represent it by a purely angular \\(X\\); adding an \\(L_0\\)-multiple changes no contraction. A common unit complex multiplier makes the value real at \\(v=T\\). Truncating at sufficiently high \\(J\\) gives the residual bounds; each output differentiation costs at most one positive power of \\(\\lambda_T\\).</p><p>For the exterior realization, parameterize beam centers \\(Y\\) by the three launch coordinates \\(w=(x,\\theta)\\). Start complex spatial phase\n\\[\nv(0,w)+\\tfrac i2|w-Y|^2\n\\]\nwith temporal root agreeing with \\(dv\\) at the center. Propagate finite complex phase jets along Proposition 6 rays. If \\(P_c,Q_c\\) are position and momentum differentials of the complex gradient graph and \\(M_c\\) is the initial Hessian, symplecticity gives\n\\[\nP_c^*Q_c-Q_c^*P_c=2i\\,\\operatorname{Im}M_c,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Im}(Q_cP_c^{-1})\n=P_c^{-*}(\\operatorname{Im}M_c)P_c^{-1}&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe first identity controls \\(P_c^{-1}\\) by \\(Q_c\\); the second controls the inverse imaginary Hessian by \\(P_c\\) and the initial inverse. All have subexponential bounds through real caustics. Finite Taylor composition and reversion use smooth jets at real points.</p><p>Central amplitudes transport by tensor parallel transport times the inverse square root of the density ratio determined by\n\\[\n\\sqrt{|\\det g|}\\,k^{t_e}\\det P_c.\n\\]\nThe propagator and inverse are subexponential on every subsegment, and higher jets satisfy lower-jet-source equations. Impose wave and gauge transports in a sufficiently high finite Taylor quotient. Their compatibility is the conjugated Bianchi identity. Contraction \\(Kp=p(k,\\cdot)\\) has the right inverse\n\\[\nR_kf=l\\otimes f+f\\otimes l-f(k)l\\otimes l,\\qquad l(k)=1.\n\\]\nThe nonzero pairing is invertible in formal jets, fixing forced gauge jets and leaving kernel jets free.</p><p>Superpose with center measure \\((\\lambda/2\\pi)^{3/2}dY\\). On the unique entry collar the center projection is a diffeomorphism. Changing centers to positions and taking Gaussian moments produces the real-phase series in integer inverse powers. At each order the new central value enters with coefficient one, while its derivatives first enter one inverse power later because odd moments vanish. For each fixed \\(T\\), an auxiliary \\(\\lambda\\to\\infty\\) limit in the residual identities shows that resulting coefficients satisfy exactly the real-phase wave and gauge transports. After lower orders match, the remaining order-\\(j\\) launch discrepancy lies in \\(\\ker K_v\\). Cancel it by choosing the free kernel value and extend its jets by \\(\\operatorname{Id}-R_kK\\). Noncharacteristic transport then matches all required normal jets; no independent value-and-normal prescription is imposed on one free datum.</p><p>A finite budget is explicit: to match coefficients through \\(J\\) to total final order \\(m\\), assign\n\\[\nm_j=m+2(J-j).\n\\]\nDifferentiating coefficient \\(j\\)’s first-order transverse transport \\(m_j-1\\) times uses at most \\(m_j+1\\) derivatives of coefficient \\(j-1\\), available since \\(m_{j-1}=m_j+2\\). Gauge forcing is first order and fits the same budget.</p><p>The auxiliary-frequency argument identifies coefficients, but actual diagonal accuracy follows from finite remainder estimates. At fixed degrees, coefficient, center-change, Hessian, inverse-Hessian, and pairing jets cost \\(e^{o(T)}\\). A transverse Taylor error of order \\(d\\) gains \\(\\lambda_T^{-d/2}e^{o(T)}\\) after Gaussian integration. Choose degrees backwards from the final differentiated residual and matching orders. One may first restrict to distances \\(\\lambda_T^{-1/2+\\epsilon_2}\\); the complementary Gaussian tail is negligible. This gives the required error at \\(\\lambda_T\\).</p><p>Realize jets with tube cutoffs of radius\n\\[\nr_T=e^{-b_pT},\\qquad 0&lt;2b_p&lt;\\zeta.\n\\]\nOn the annulus, higher phase terms are less than half the positive quadratic imaginary part for large \\(T\\), using \\(r_Te^{o(T)}\\to0\\) and the inverse-Hessian bound. Hence\n\\[\ne^{-\\lambda_T\\operatorname{Im}\\varphi}\n\\le\\exp\\!\\left[-c\\exp\\{(\\zeta-2b_p)T-o(T)\\}\\right].\n\\]\nThis dominates fixed cutoff-derivative losses and every required inverse power. Entry uniqueness excludes additional traces. Stop tubes beyond the strict deeper exit; their bridge support lies in the stated return shell.</p><p>Absolute center integration costs \\(\\lambda_T^{3/2}\\), and \\(N_b\\) differentiations cost at most \\(\\lambda_T^{N_b}e^{o(T)}\\). Additional formal coefficients have nonpositive frequency powers, so improving accuracy changes thresholds and constants but not the positive output powers.</p><p>Every operation is linear and homogeneous in \\(A_T\\). Thus replacing \\(7\\kappa/8\\) by any \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\) proves the original Corollary 4.8. For example,\n\\[\nA_T\\lambda_T^{-M_1}e^{o(T)}\\le e^{-4\\kappa T}\n\\]\nfollows after choosing \\(\\alpha+M_1\\zeta&gt;4\\kappa\\) and then \\(T\\) large. Output and base buffers are fixed before \\(\\zeta\\); formal accuracy is increased only afterwards. The companion’s final tidal or \\(C^2\\)-inextendibility conclusions are not inputs.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 5.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After fixing finite derivative buffers, choosing \\(\\zeta>0\\) sufficiently small, and then finite formal accuracies sufficiently large, there are smooth positive complete vacuum data \\(d_T^\\pm\\in\\mathcal D\\) satisfying …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(d\\in\\mathcal U\\), a selected branch, its packet sequence from Proposition 8, and\n  \\[\n  A_T=e^{-\\alpha T},\\qquad \\alpha=7\\kappa/8,\\qquad\n  \\lambda_T=e^{\\zeta T}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix finite \\(m\\ge10\\), finite entry order \\(r\\), loss \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), and \\(0&lt;\\nu_0&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the entry collar, normalized arrays, and full norm \\(\\mathfrak J_{r,T}\\) defined in Theorem 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After fixing finite derivative buffers, choosing \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\) sufficiently small, and then finite formal accuracies sufficiently large, there are smooth positive complete vacuum data \\(d_T^\\pm\\in\\mathcal D\\) satisfying\n\\[\np_m(d_T^\\pm-d)\\to0.\n\\]\nThey preserve the other end, connecting region, and the protected ball\n\\[\nB_T=\\{|z-z_*|&lt;\\rho_T\\},\\qquad\nz_*=C_e(t_T),\\qquad\n\\rho_T=(1-2\\beta)t_T.\n\\]\nThe protected region contains every fixed compact set for large \\(T\\). Every differentiated weight-one metric and weight-two second-form bound is finite. No outer cutoff is applied to their correction tails.</p><p>Exact exterior vacuum evolutions with common target \\(g\\) reach the needed entry collar, and\n\\[\n\\mathfrak J_{r,T}(F_T^\\pm)\\le A_T^2e^{\\eta T},\n\\qquad\n\\mathfrak J_{r,T}(F_T^+-F_T^-)\\le A_T^3e^{\\eta T},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nH_T^\\pm=\\widehat{g_T^\\pm-g},\\qquad F_T^\\pm=H_T^\\pm\\mp q_T.\n\\]\nAll changes, packets, and errors vanish on the earlier needed entry interval \\(v&lt;(1-\\nu_0)T\\). Order \\(r=61\\) and simultaneous scaled linear residual \\(e^{-4\\kappa T}\\) through order \\(60\\) are permitted. The same holds in the exchanged branch preparation.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(\\beta\\) small with \\(1-2\\beta-s_*&gt;0\\). Proposition 6 places the induced beam seed strictly outside \\(B_T\\), with tube margins. On \\(t_e=0\\), induce data from \\(g+\\tau h_T^B\\), \\(-1\\le\\tau\\le1\\), and retain \\(d\\) off the beam.</p><p>On the selected end rescale component functions by\n\\[\ny'=\\frac{z-z_*}{\\rho_T},\\qquad\n\\widetilde h(y')=h(z_*+\\rho_Ty'),\\qquad\n\\widetilde K(y')=\\rho_TK(z_*+\\rho_Ty').\n\\]\nBoth constraint components acquire factor \\(\\rho_T^2\\). Choose fixed\n\\[\n\\frac{s_*}{1-2\\beta}&lt;r_0&lt;r_1&lt;1.\n\\]\nFor \\(|y'|\\ge r_0\\), \\(|z|\\asymp\\rho_T|y'|\\), and the fixed datum’s symbol bounds give\n\\[\n|\\partial_{y'}^j(\\widetilde h-\\delta)|\n\\le C_j\\rho_T^{-1}|y'|^{-1-j},\\qquad\n|\\partial_{y'}^j\\widetilde K|\n\\le C_j\\rho_T^{-1}|y'|^{-2-j}.\n\\]\nCut background deviations to zero between \\(r_0,r_1\\), leave them unchanged outside \\(r_1\\), and add the beam seed outside the unit sphere. Denote the artificial data \\(D(\\tau)\\), with \\(D_\\pm=D(\\pm1)\\). Outside the unit ball \\(D(0)\\) is the actual vacuum datum. The exterior defect, extended by zero inside, is smooth and compactly supported because the beam vanishes near the unit sphere and beyond its shell.</p><p>Fix a base solve order \\(s\\) and a small preliminary loss \\(\\epsilon\\). Finite-jet induction of hypersurface data and Proposition 8 give\n\\[\n\\|D_\\pm-(\\delta,0)\\|_{X^s}\n\\le C_s\\rho_T^{-1}+A_Te^{\\epsilon T},\n\\qquad\n\\|D_+-D_-\\|_{X^s}\\le A_Te^{\\epsilon T},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|\\phi_\\pm\\|_{Y^s}\\le A_T^2e^{\\epsilon T},\n\\qquad\n\\|\\phi_+-\\phi_-\\|_{Y^s}\\le A_T^3e^{\\epsilon T}.\n\\]\nIndeed Gauss–Codazzi expresses the constraints as normal Einstein components. Their linearization is controlled by the linear reduced residual and one derivative of the gauge defect. Thus an \\(s\\)-jet constraint estimate requests gauge-defect jets through \\(s+1\\), fixed before choosing \\(\\zeta\\). Taylor expansion gives\n\\[\n\\phi(\\tau)=\\tau\\ell_T+\\tau^2Q_T+\\tau^3R_T(\\tau),\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n\\|\\ell_T\\|_{Y^s}\\le A_T\\lambda_T^{-M_1}e^{o(T)},\\quad\n\\|Q_T\\|_{Y^s}\\le A_T^2\\lambda_T^{P_s}e^{o(T)},\\quad\n\\sup_{|\\tau|\\le1}\\|R_T(\\tau)\\|_{Y^s}\n\\le A_T^3\\lambda_T^{P_s'}e^{o(T)}.\n\\]\nThe quadratic term is identical at both signs. Choose \\(\\zeta\\) small for the fixed positive powers, then \\(M_1\\) large to put the linear term below cubic size. This also handles nonlinear dependence of the induced second form on lapse and normal.</p><p>Lemma 9.1 gives, using one common inverse,\n\\[\n\\|v_\\pm\\|_{X^s}\\le C_sA_T^2e^{\\epsilon T},\\qquad\n\\|v_+-v_-\\|_{X^s}\\le C_sA_T^3e^{2\\epsilon T}.\n\\]\nUnscale outside the protected ball, retain \\(d\\) inside, and leave the rest of the bridge unchanged. Vanishing on the inner side makes the gluing smooth. The artificial nonvacuum interior is discarded and never evolved.</p><p>For \\(s\\ge m+3\\), scaled Sobolev embedding controls the required weighted pointwise derivatives. Unscaling either correction costs at most \\(C_m\\rho_T\\), giving\n\\[\np_m(d_T^\\pm-d)\n\\le\\operatorname{poly}(T)\n\\left(A_T\\lambda_T^{m+5/2}e^{o(T)}\n+A_T^2e^{\\epsilon T}\\right).\n\\]\nThe extra seed derivative enters the second form. Small \\(\\zeta\\) makes this tend to zero, preserving positivity and completeness; all-order symbols give membership in \\(\\mathcal D\\). Moreover\n\\[\nB\\bigl(0,(1-2\\beta-s_*)t_T-O(1)\\bigr)\\subset B_T,\n\\]\nso fixed compact causal shadows are protected. No outer correction cutoff is taken.</p><p>Initialize reduced exterior evolution with corrected physical data, prescribing lapse and shift equal to those of \\(g\\pm h_T^B\\). These determine metric values and first spatial derivatives; the second form determines temporal derivatives of spatial metric entries. The remaining derivatives satisfy \\(\\Upsilon=0\\). If \\(x_\\nu=\\partial_{t_e}\\widetilde g_{0\\nu}\\), the lowered contracted Christoffel expression\n\\[\n\\widetilde g^{\\alpha\\beta}\n(\\partial_\\alpha\\widetilde g_{\\beta\\nu}\n-\\tfrac12\\partial_\\nu\\widetilde g_{\\alpha\\beta})\n\\]\nhas coefficient \\(\\widetilde g^{00}/2\\) on \\(x_0\\) in the \\(\\nu=0\\) equation, with \\(x_i\\)-terms canceled, and coefficient \\(\\widetilde g^{00}\\) on \\(x_i\\) in the \\(\\nu=i\\) equations. Uniform spacelikeness makes this algebraic solve uniformly invertible.</p><p>Recover higher temporal jets from the reduced equation by solving for \\(\\partial_{t_e}^2\\widetilde g\\), then differentiating. Thus each finite full initial jet is a smooth finite-jet function of corrected data and prescribed lapse/shift. Linear discrepancies are residual-controlled, quadratic discrepancies are even in the sign, and changing the coefficient multiplying a quadratic correction costs an extra \\(A_Te^{\\epsilon T}\\). For\n\\[\nf_T^\\pm=g_T^\\pm-g\\mp h_T^B,\n\\]\nthe complete initial jets satisfy\n\\[\n\\|f_T^\\pm\\|_{\\mathrm{init}}\\le A_T^2e^{c\\epsilon T},\n\\qquad\n\\|f_T^+-f_T^-\\|_{\\mathrm{init}}\\le A_T^3e^{c\\epsilon T}.\n\\]\nOn the protected side all gauge Cauchy jets agree exactly with the background.</p><p>Contracted Bianchi gives a homogeneous wave equation for \\(\\Upsilon\\). Its initial value is zero by construction, and the exact normal Einstein constraints give its zero normal derivative. Hence every resulting reduced solution is vacuum wherever it exists.</p><p>Use the exterior computing domain through \\(t_T+C_2\\), with strict deeper exit and outer cutoff\n\\[\n|y|&lt;R_{\\mathrm{out}}(t_e),\\qquad\nR_{\\mathrm{out}}(t_e)\n=C_{\\mathrm{out}}(1+T)+V_{\\mathrm{out}}(t_T+C_2-t_e),\n\\]\nwhere \\(V_{\\mathrm{out}}\\) exceeds the causal radial speed. This is only a computing restriction; the full initial tail remains.</p><p>If a causal path from changed support first reaches large fixed co-radius \\(R\\) at \\(\\tau\\le t_T\\), its prior lab speed is at most \\(1+\\delta_R\\), \\(\\delta_R\\to0\\). The protected ball and drift give\n\\[\n(1-2\\beta)t_T\n\\le(1+\\delta_R)\\tau+s_*(t_T-\\tau)+O(R),\n\\]\nhence\n\\[\nt_T-\\tau\\le\n\\frac{2\\beta+\\delta_R}{1+\\delta_R-s_*}t_T+O_R(1).\n\\]\nFirst take \\(R\\) large and then \\(\\beta\\) small to make this at most \\(\\theta T+O_R(1)\\), for any prescribed \\(\\theta&gt;0\\). The beam and residual have the same late compact-radius support. Since\n\\[\nv&lt;(1-\\nu_0)T\n\\Longrightarrow\nt_e\\le t_T-\\frac{\\nu_0}{C_I}T+O(1),\n\\]\nchoose \\(\\theta&lt;\\nu_0/(2C_I)\\). Finite propagation gives exact earlier entry agreement. Bounded middle and unselected pieces have protected dependence regions, and the deeper face is outflow.</p><p>In lab components write the reduced change equation\n\\[\n\\mathfrak R_e(H)_n\n=(\\mathcal L_eH)_n\n+B^{\\mu\\nu}(H)\\partial_\\mu\\partial_\\nu H_n\n+\\mathcal Q_n(H,\\partial H),\n\\quad\nB(H)=(g+H)^{-1}-g^{-1}.\n\\]\nNonlinearities begin at degree two and have total field derivative order at most two. At fixed order,\n\\[\n\\|\\mathfrak R_e(\\pm h_T^B)\\|_{H^j_{\\mathrm{mix}}}\n\\le e^{o(T)}\n\\left(A_T\\lambda_T^{-M_1}+A_T^2\\lambda_T^{P_j}\\right).\n\\]</p><p>Choose finite exterior order \\(k_e\\ge16\\). Let \\(E_j^\\pm\\) be square roots of summed scalar-stress fluxes for order-\\(j\\) mixed derivatives of \\(f_T^\\pm\\), using the actual metric, future slice normal, and fixed small positive mass \\(\\mu\\). These are uniformly equivalent to ordinary derivative energies. Bootstrap by \\(e^{-\\alpha T}\\). Retain the exact perturbed principal operator. Every remaining product has an error and another packet/error factor, total derivative count at most \\(j+2\\), and at most \\(j+1\\) on any error. Put the highest error derivative in \\(L^2\\), others in supremum using the fixed high bootstrap buffer. Packet factors have fixed base bounds. No optical lapse conversion occurs.</p><p>Highest principal commutators and stress deformation use first lab metric derivatives; tensor first- and zero-order terms use at most second derivatives. Choose \\(\\mu\\) small, then \\(R\\) large, to make same-level growth arbitrarily small outside \\(R\\). Inside it the rate is bounded independently of \\(R\\); additional coefficient hits multiply strictly lower levels with \\(e^{o(T)}\\) coefficients. Outflow fluxes are favorable. Late support sees the compact rate for only \\(\\theta T+O_R(1)\\).</p><p>Thus one common nonnegative rate \\(r_e\\), for both signs and the background equation, gives\n\\[\n(E_j^\\pm)'\\le r_eE_j^\\pm\n+b_T\\sum_{\\ell&lt;j}E_\\ell^\\pm+S_j,\n\\]\n\\[\nR_e^{\\mathrm{int}}(t)=\\int_0^tr_e,\\qquad\nR_e^{\\mathrm{int}}(t_T+C_2)\\le\\epsilon_eT,\\qquad\n\\int_0^{t_T+C_2}b_T=e^{o(T)}.\n\\]\nConjugating by \\(e^{-R_e^{\\mathrm{int}}}\\) leaves a strictly lower triangular system. Its time-ordered series terminates after \\(k_e\\) drops, so the propagator on every subinterval costs\n\\[\ne^{R_e^{\\mathrm{int}}(t)-R_e^{\\mathrm{int}}(s)+o(T)}.\n\\]\nInitial bounds and Duhamel give\n\\[\n\\max_{j\\le k_e}E_j^\\pm(t_e)\\le A_T^2e^{\\epsilon_iT}\n\\]\nwith arbitrarily small \\(\\epsilon_i&gt;0\\). This improves the bootstrap, preserves temporal and outflow margins, and yields ordinary reduced-wave continuation through the finite domain. Higher smooth norms propagate separately.</p><p>Subtract the two error equations with the same background \\(\\mathcal L_e\\) on the left. Pure quadratic beam forcing cancels exactly. Remaining pure-beam terms are cubic; all others contain an error and another field factor. At orders at least a two-derivative and Sobolev buffer below \\(k_e\\),\n\\[\n\\|\\mathcal L_e(f_T^+-f_T^-)\\|_{H^j_{\\mathrm{mix}}}\n\\le e^{c\\epsilon_iT+o(T)}(A_T^3+A_T^4)\n+A_T\\lambda_T^{-M_1}e^{o(T)}.\n\\]\nKnown individual higher derivatives pay for second error derivatives. Apply the same late-support background propagator and cubic initial jets to obtain the cubic odd bound.</p><p>Transform full jets to the fixed optical entry coordinates and restrict to \\(S_T\\). Coordinate and normalized-frame jets cost \\(e^{o(T)}\\), Sobolev embedding uses two spare spatial derivatives, and entry volume costs only a polynomial. Proposition 8’s full normal-jet matching adds only inverse-accuracy error, proving the stated entry inequalities.</p><p>For the order of choices, take\n\\[\nk_e\\ge\\max\\{16,r+10\\},\\qquad\ns\\ge\\max\\{m+3,k_e+8\\},\n\\]\nthen a larger fixed beam base buffer covering constraints, normal recursion, products, and traces; include one extra gauge-defect derivative for every constraint order. Allocate preliminary losses below \\(\\eta\\). Fix mass, far-rate tolerance, radius, and shell tolerance to meet both growth and \\(\\nu_0\\). Choose \\(\\zeta\\) for all fixed positive powers and \\((m+5/2)\\zeta&lt;\\alpha\\). Only then choose inverse accuracy and formal degrees to put residuals below cubic size and \\(e^{-4\\kappa T}\\). Finally take \\(T\\) large. Smoothness at all orders comes from the single base-order constraint solve, not from choosing one \\(\\zeta\\) to ensure convergence in every seminorm simultaneously.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For sufficiently small \\(\\epsilon_s,b_2\\), there are corrections \\(v_\\pm\\), using the same \\(\\mathcal K\\), supported in \\(\\{|y'|\\ge1\\}\\), with \\( \\Phi(D_\\pm+v_\\pm)=0\\quad(|y'|\\ge1), \\qquad \\|v_\\pm\\|_{X^s}\\le C_sb_2, \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For Cartesian tensors on \\(\\mathbb R^3\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\|f\\|_{\\mathcal H_\\ell^j}\n  =\\|f\\|_{H^j(B_2)}\n  +\\sup_{R\\ge1}R^\\ell\\|f(R\\cdot)\\|_{H^j(1&lt;|y'|&lt;2)},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  X^s=\\mathcal H_1^{s+2}\\oplus\\mathcal H_2^{s+1},\n  \\qquad Y^s=\\mathcal H_4^s.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Phi\\) be the vacuum constraint map and\n  \\[\n  P_{\\mathrm{flat}}(q,p)\n  =(\\partial_i\\partial_jq_{ij}-\\Delta q_{ii},\n  \\partial_jp_{ij}-\\partial_ip_{jj}).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The inverse [20, Lemma D.2] is one linear operator \\(\\mathcal K:Y^s\\to X^s\\), bounded for every integer \\(s\\ge3\\), defined on all of \\(Y^s\\), with\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{supp}z\\subset\\{|y'|\\ge1\\}\n  \\Longrightarrow\n  P_{\\mathrm{flat}}\\mathcal Kz=z,\\quad\n  \\operatorname{supp}\\mathcal Kz\\subset\\{|y'|\\ge1\\}.\n  \\]\n  Its rotation and positive argument-dilation conjugates are smooth in operator norm.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(s\\ge3\\). Let \\(D_\\pm=(\\delta+a_\\pm,b_\\pm)\\) be smooth positive data with all-order symbol decay \\((-1,-2)\\) and\n  \\[\n  \\|D_\\pm-(\\delta,0)\\|_{X^s}\\le\\epsilon_s.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let smooth compact sources \\(\\phi_\\pm\\), supported in \\(\\{|y'|\\ge1\\}\\), equal \\(\\Phi(D_\\pm)\\) there. Set \\(b_2=\\max_\\pm\\|\\phi_\\pm\\|_{Y^s}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently small \\(\\epsilon_s,b_2\\), there are corrections \\(v_\\pm\\), using the same \\(\\mathcal K\\), supported in \\(\\{|y'|\\ge1\\}\\), with\n\\[\n\\Phi(D_\\pm+v_\\pm)=0\\quad(|y'|\\ge1),\n\\qquad\n\\|v_\\pm\\|_{X^s}\\le C_sb_2,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\|v_+-v_-\\|_{X^s}\n\\le C_s\\left(\n\\|\\phi_+-\\phi_-\\|_{Y^s}\n+\\|D_+-D_-\\|_{X^s}b_2\n\\right).\n\\]\nThe corrections are smooth across the unit sphere and have all differentiated symbol bounds \\((-1,-2)\\). Only the base norms need be small; \\(D_\\pm\\) need not be vacuum in the artificial interior.</p>", "proof": "<p>Set\n\\[\nN_D(v)=\\Phi(D+v)-\\Phi(D)-P_{\\mathrm{flat}}v.\n\\]\nThe subtraction is essential: a general \\(X^s\\) input has flat constraint terms of weight three, and no claim is made that the full constraint map takes an arbitrary \\(X^s\\)-neighborhood into \\(Y^s\\).</p><p>After cancellation the scalar products have types\n\\[\na\\,\\partial^2a,\\quad(\\partial a)^2,\\quad b^2,\n\\]\nand momentum products have types\n\\[\na\\,\\partial b,\\quad(\\partial a)b,\n\\]\nwith bounded smooth inverse-metric coefficients and at least one correction factor. The weight budgets are \\(1+3=4\\) and \\(2+2=4\\). Rescaled-annulus and unit-ball Sobolev multiplication gives\n\\[\n\\|N_D(v)-N_D(w)\\|_{Y^s}\n\\le C_s(\\|D-(\\delta,0)\\|_{X^s}+b)\\|v-w\\|_{X^s},\n\\]\nfor \\(\\|v\\|,\\|w\\|\\le b\\), and the mixed second differential gives\n\\[\n\\|N_{D_+}(v)-N_{D_-}(v)\\|_{Y^s}\n\\le C_s\\|D_+-D_-\\|_{X^s}\\|v\\|_{X^s}.\n\\]\nInverse-metric differentiation preserves these estimates on a uniformly positive small ball.</p><p>Solve for both signs\n\\[\nz_\\pm=-\\phi_\\pm-N_{D_\\pm}(\\mathcal Kz_\\pm),\n\\qquad v_\\pm=\\mathcal Kz_\\pm\n\\]\non the common ball \\(\\|z\\|_{Y^s}\\le2b_2\\), with contraction constant at most \\(1/2\\). Starting at zero preserves exterior support by locality and the inverse property. The fixed points solve the constraints. Subtraction and absorption of the half-Lipschitz term give the signed estimate.</p><p>To regularize these same fixed points, let \\(U_\\vartheta,V_\\vartheta\\) be rotation and positive argument-dilation actions on \\(X^s,Y^s\\), including tensor component rotations. Put\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_\\vartheta=U_\\vartheta\\mathcal KV_\\vartheta^{-1},\n\\quad\n\\phi_\\vartheta=V_\\vartheta\\phi,\n\\quad\nN_\\vartheta(w)=V_\\vartheta N_D(U_\\vartheta^{-1}w).\n\\]\nThe inverse family is norm-smooth; all-order symbols of \\(D\\) and compact smooth \\(\\phi\\) make the remaining expressions smooth. Parameter differentiation of the explicit weighted products differentiates known coefficients and explicit scale/component factors without demanding extra unknown derivatives.</p><p>On the full space consider\n\\[\nw+\\mathcal K_\\vartheta\\phi_\\vartheta\n+\\mathcal K_\\vartheta N_\\vartheta(w)=0.\n\\]\nIts derivative is \\(\\operatorname{Id}+\\mathcal K_\\vartheta DN_\\vartheta(w)\\), invertible by a Neumann series. The implicit-function theorem gives a smooth parameter branch. The algebraically transformed fixed point \\(U_\\vartheta v\\) lies in a common uniqueness ball by boundedness of the actions, hence equals this branch. Norm continuity of the unknown orbit is not assumed. The all-input definition of \\(\\mathcal K\\) is needed because dilation moves the support boundary.</p><p>Distributional differentiation gives every iterate of rotations\n\\[\n\\Omega_{ij}=y_i'\\partial_j-y_j'\\partial_i\n\\]\nand dilation \\(E=y'\\cdot\\partial\\) in \\(X^s\\). Component rotations contribute removable zero-order matrices. Since\n\\[\n|y'|^2\\partial_i=y_i'E+\\sum_jy_j'\\Omega_{ji},\n\\]\nthese span scaled coordinate derivatives on exterior annuli and near the unit sphere. Scaled Sobolev embedding gives smoothness and all differentiated symbol bounds there. The correction vanishes inside the sphere, so is smooth across it. Base smallness preserves positivity; higher bounds need not be small.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Proposition 6.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For sufficiently small fixed \\(\\zeta>0\\) and sufficiently large \\(T\\), both exact entry data have smooth vacuum evolutions \\(g_T^\\pm\\) on \\(\\Omega_T\\), in target-\\(g\\) wave-map gauge.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the background, branch, and coordinates of Propositions 4–8.</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  W_t=L_0+L_1=\\partial_t+b^A\\partial_A,\\qquad\n  W_x=L_1-L_0=\\partial_x-b^A\\partial_A,\\qquad\n  Z_A=\\sqrt a\\,\\partial_A.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the strict retained domain\n  \\[\n  \\Omega_T=\n  \\left\\{\n  0\\le t&lt;L_T,\\quad\n  u+\\frac{c_0t}{T}&lt;u_2,\\quad\n  v+\\frac{c_0t}{T}&lt;T+c_2\n  \\right\\},\n  \\qquad\n  L_T=\\frac{T+u_*}{2}+c_0,\n  \\]\n  with fixed strict observation margins and construction on a slight enlargement.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(C^r_{\\mathrm{mix}}\\) and \\(H^r_{\\mathrm{mix}}\\) include ordinary mixed spacetime derivatives through order \\(r\\), with slice \\(L^2(dx\\,d\\operatorname{vol}_\\gamma)\\) for the latter.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(A=A_T=e^{-7\\kappa T/8}\\), \\(\\alpha=7\\kappa/8\\), \\(\\lambda=\\lambda_T=e^{\\zeta T}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;\\kappa/32\\) and a prescribed finite data seminorm. Use sufficiently high finite entry/output buffers, sufficiently small exterior losses and late-support fraction, and sufficiently accurate linear residuals in Proposition 9.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently small fixed \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\) and sufficiently large \\(T\\), both exact entry data have smooth vacuum evolutions \\(g_T^\\pm\\) on \\(\\Omega_T\\), in target-\\(g\\) wave-map gauge. If\n\\[\nH_T^\\pm=\\widehat{g_T^\\pm-g},\\qquad\nF_T^\\pm=H_T^\\pm\\mp q_T,\\qquad\nF_T^{\\mathrm{odd}}=F_T^+-F_T^-,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\|F_T^\\pm\\|_{C^{30}_{\\mathrm{mix}}(\\Omega_T)}\n\\le e^{(\\kappa-2\\alpha+\\delta)T},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|F_T^{\\mathrm{odd}}\\|_{C^2_{\\mathrm{mix}}(\\Omega_T)}\n\\le e^{(\\kappa-3\\alpha+\\delta)T}\n+e^{(2\\kappa-4\\alpha+\\delta)T}.\n\\]\nChanges and errors are supported in \\(v\\ge(1-\\nu_0)T-o(1)\\); slices stay spacelike and side faces strict future exits. For some \\(c&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\|H_T^\\pm\\|_{C^{30}_{\\mathrm{mix}}}\n\\le e^{-(\\kappa/2+c)T},\n\\]\nand, at every fixed order in use,\n\\[\ne^{o(T)}\n\\left(\n\\|F_T^{\\mathrm{odd}}\\|_{C^2_{\\mathrm{mix}}}\n+\\sum_\\pm\\|H_T^\\pm\\|_{C^2_{\\mathrm{mix}}}^2\n\\right)=o(A_T).\n\\]\nNo high seminorm of the fixed background need be small.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a small symmetric relative array \\(H\\), let \\(h[H]\\) be its physical tensor, \\(g_H=g+h[H]\\), and\n\\[\nG_{ab}=g(\\widehat e_a,\\widehat e_b)\n=\n\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-1&amp;0\\\\-1&amp;0&amp;0\\\\0&amp;0&amp;\\gamma_{AB}\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad Q(H)=G+H.\n\\]\nWith \\(Z_a=\\sqrt a\\,\\widehat e_a=J_a{}^\\mu\\partial_\\mu\\),\n\\[\nag_H^{\\mu\\nu}=Q(H)^{ab}J_a{}^\\mu J_b{}^\\nu.\n\\]\nThus angular second-derivative coefficients retain \\(a\\), radial-angular coefficients retain \\(\\sqrt a\\), and inversion of \\(Q\\) introduces no negative lapse power.</p><p>The connection difference is\n\\[\nC^\\lambda{}_{\\mu\\nu}\n=\\tfrac12g_H^{\\lambda\\rho}\n(\\nabla_\\mu h_{\\nu\\rho}\n+\\nabla_\\nu h_{\\mu\\rho}\n-\\nabla_\\rho h_{\\mu\\nu}),\n\\]\nand the normalized array of \\(\\sqrt a\\,\\nabla_{\\widehat e_a}h[H]\\) is\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_aH=Z_aH-V_a^TH-HV_a.\n\\]\nA second covariant derivative multiplied by \\(a\\) involves \\(Z_aZ_bH,Z_aH,H\\), and differentiation of \\(a^{-1/2}\\) creates \\(Z_a\\log a\\), not an extra inverse lapse. Proposition 7 controls scaled differentiated connections and curvature, while fixed ordinary jets retain\n\\[\nD^Ia=a\\,e^{o(T)},\\qquad\nD^I\\sqrt a=\\sqrt a\\,e^{o(T)},\\qquad\nD^I\\partial_vb=a\\,e^{o(T)}.\n\\]</p><p>Normalize the reduced array equation so that its principal part is \\(ag_H^{\\mu\\nu}D_\\mu D_\\nu H\\). Its linearization \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathfrak R(H)_n\n=(\\mathcal LH)_n+B^{\\mu\\nu}(H)D_\\mu D_\\nu H_n\n+\\mathcal Q_n(H,DH),\n\\quad\nB^{\\mu\\nu}(H)=a(g_H^{\\mu\\nu}-g^{\\mu\\nu}).\n\\]\nHere \\(B(0)=0\\), and \\(\\mathcal Q\\) begins at degree two with total field derivative count at most two; fixed coefficient jets are \\(e^{o(T)}\\). For every fixed comparison order,\n\\[\n\\|\\mathfrak R(\\pm q_T)\\|_{H^j_{\\mathrm{mix}}}\n\\le e^{o(T)}(A\\lambda^{-M_1}+A^2\\lambda^{P_j})\n\\le A^2e^{\\eta T}.\n\\]\nRequire also \\(\\|\\mathcal Lq_T\\|_{H^{60}_{\\mathrm{mix}}}\\le e^{-4\\kappa T}\\).</p><p>For one sign write \\(q=\\pm q_T\\), \\(F=F_T^\\pm\\), \\(g'=g_{q+F}\\). Subtraction gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal LF+B^{\\mu\\nu}(q+F)D_\\mu D_\\nu F\n=-\\mathfrak R(q)\n-\\bigl(B^{\\mu\\nu}(q+F)-B^{\\mu\\nu}(q)\\bigr)D_\\mu D_\\nu q\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\bigl(\\mathcal Q(q+F,Dq+DF)-\\mathcal Q(q,Dq)\\bigr).\n\\]\nAfter \\(j\\) ordinary differentiations retain the exact principal \\(ag'^{\\mu\\nu}D_\\mu D_\\nu\\) on each \\(D^IF_n\\). Every other perturbative product has an error and another packet/error factor, total field derivative count at most \\(j+2\\), and no error with more than \\(j+1\\) derivatives.</p><p>The constant slot actions from Proposition 7 are isolated using\n\\[\ne^{2c_nx}L_0e^{-2c_nx}=L_0+c_n,\\qquad\ne^{2c_nx}L_1e^{-2c_nx}=L_1-c_n.\n\\]\nUse the diagonal scalar operator\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{n,g'}f=e^{2c_nx}a\\Box_{g'}^{\\mathrm{scal}}(e^{-2c_nx}f).\n\\]\nFor the background, its difference from \\(\\mathcal L\\) has bounded first- and zero-order coefficients on \\(L_if,Z_Af,f\\), tending to zero in the bulk. Replacing \\(g\\) by \\(g'\\) and passing to divergence form creates only the counted perturbative terms. In particular\n\\[\n\\sqrt{|\\det g'|}\n=\\sqrt{|\\det g|}\n\\left|\\frac{\\det Q(q+F)}{\\det G}\\right|^{1/2},\n\\]\nso no extra inverse lapse is introduced.</p><p>For \\(f=D^IF_n\\), put\n\\[\n\\widetilde f=e^{-2c_nx}f,\\qquad\n\\mu^2=\\frac{\\epsilon_m^2}{2a},\\qquad\nX_n=e^{4c_nx}W_t.\n\\]\nThe massive scalar stress flux at \\(g'=g\\) is exactly\n\\[\n\\frac12\\int\n\\left(\n|W_tf|^2+|(W_x-2c_n)f|^2\n+2a\\gamma^{AB}\\partial_Af\\partial_Bf\n+\\epsilon_m^2|f|^2\n\\right)\\,dx\\,d\\operatorname{vol}_\\gamma.\n\\]\nFor small relative changes the exact flux is uniformly equivalent by finite-dimensional comparison in \\((W_t,W_x,Z_A)\\), also proving spacelike slices and future-timelike \\(W_t\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal E_j\\) sum these exact fluxes at exactly order \\(j\\), over components, multiindices, and angular localizations, and \\(E_j=\\sqrt{\\mathcal E_j}\\). The mass terms give\n\\[\n\\|F(t)\\|_{C^r_{\\mathrm{mix}}}\n\\le C_{r,\\epsilon_m}\\sum_{j=0}^{r+2}E_j(t).\n\\]\nThis uses ordinary spatial \\(H^2\\hookrightarrow L^\\infty\\) for each mixed derivative, with fixed-length overlapping radial intervals and a positive minimum slice length. There is no angular lapse conversion.</p><p>Bootstrap \\(E_j\\le e^{-5\\kappa T/8}\\) for \\(0\\le j\\le60\\). Full entry jets through \\(61\\) give \\(E_j(0)\\le A^2e^{\\eta T}\\). Cone comparison yields\n\\[\ndu/dt,\\ dv/dt\\ge-Ce^{-5\\kappa T/8+o(T)}.\n\\]\nThe tilted side functions therefore increase strictly on future causal curves, and finite propagation gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{supp}H_T^\\pm\\cup\\operatorname{supp}F_T^\\pm\n\\subset\\{v\\ge(1-\\nu_0)T-\\omega_T\\},\n\\quad\n\\omega_T=CTe^{-5\\kappa T/8+o(T)}=o(1).\n\\]</p><p>For background commutators, the scaled scalar second-coordinate part is\n\\[\n-2\\partial_u\\partial_v-2b^A\\partial_A\\partial_v\n+a\\gamma^{AB}\\partial_A\\partial_B.\n\\]\nThe first coefficient is constant. One hit on \\(b\\) leaves a radial derivative of an order-\\(j\\) commuted field, controlled at the same level with bounded, bulk-small \\(Db\\). Two or more hits lower the energy level and may cost \\(e^{o(T)}\\). For the angular term,\n\\[\nD(a\\gamma^{AB})\n=a((D\\log a)\\gamma^{AB}+D\\gamma^{AB}).\n\\]\nPutting one angular derivative in the commuted field and writing the other as \\(a^{-1/2}Z_A\\) leaves \\(\\sqrt a\\) times a bounded low coefficient; multiple coefficient hits lower the level. First-order, zero-order, localization, and chart-transition commutators are treated the same way. Chart changes act on angular slots and preserve \\(c_n\\); radial brackets retain \\(\\partial_vb=aO(1)\\).</p><p>For perturbative products use at most once\n\\[\n\\|D^{j+1}F\\|_{L^2}\n\\le e^{\\kappa T/2+o(T)}\\sum_{\\ell\\le j}E_\\ell.\n\\]\nThis follows from \\(\\partial_A=a^{-1/2}Z_A\\), the radial energy and fixed mass, and \\(a^{-1/2}\\le e^{\\kappa T/2+O(1)}\\). Place the highest differentiated error in \\(L^2\\); any other error has order at most \\((j+2)/2\\le31\\) and is bounded by mass energies through \\(33\\). Packet factors are \\(A\\lambda^Pe^{o(T)}\\). The resulting coefficient is\n\\[\ne^{\\kappa T/2+o(T)}\n(e^{-5\\kappa T/8}+A\\lambda^P)\n=e^{-\\kappa T/8+o(T)}\n+e^{(-3\\kappa/8+P\\zeta)T+o(T)}\n=o(1).\n\\]\nThere are never two angular conversions.</p><p>If \\(\\mathcal C_{n,g'}f=S_n\\), the conjugated source pairing is\n\\[\n(a^{-1}e^{-2c_nx}S_n)\n(e^{4c_nx}e^{-2c_nx}W_tf)\n=a^{-1}S_nW_tf.\n\\]\nThe spacetime volume \\(2a\\sqrt{\\det\\gamma}(1+O(|H|))\\,dt\\,dx\\,d\\theta\\) cancels \\(a^{-1}\\). Hence the squared-energy source is bounded by \\(C\\|S_n\\|_{L^2}E_j\\). Off-diagonal entries are estimated in their own unconjugated variables; no ratio \\(e^{2(c_n-c_m)x}\\) occurs.</p><p>To obtain the common sharp rate, first take a fixed angular metric, \\(b=0\\), \\(a=a_0e^{-2\\kappa t}\\). The conjugated equation is\n\\[\n\\tfrac12(-f_{tt}+(\\partial_x-2c_n)^2f)+a\\Delta_\\gamma f=S_n.\n\\]\nWith \\(P=f_t\\), \\(V=(\\partial_x-2c_n)f\\), differentiating the flux gives\n\\[\n\\frac d{dt}\\mathcal E[f]\n=\\int\\left(\n-4c_nPV-2\\kappa a|d_\\theta f|_{\\gamma^{-1}}^2\n+\\epsilon_m^2fP-2S_nP\n\\right)\\,dx\\,d\\operatorname{vol}_\\gamma,\n\\]\nup to favorable side flux. The angular term is nonpositive,\n\\[\n4|c_nPV|\\le2|c_n|(|P|^2+|V|^2),\\qquad\n\\epsilon_m^2|fP|\n\\le\\tfrac{\\epsilon_m}{2}(\\epsilon_m^2|f|^2+|P|^2).\n\\]\nThe square-root rate is at most \\(2\\kappa+\\epsilon_m/2\\), independent of commutation order because \\(D\\) adds no tensor slots.</p><p>For the actual background, the relevant relative deformations converge to this model:\n\\[\nW_t\\log a=-2\\kappa+o(1),\\quad W_t\\gamma=o(1),\n\\]\n\\[\n[W_t,W_x]=-2(\\partial_vb)^A\\partial_A=o(1)Z_A,\\qquad\n[W_t,Z_A]=-\\kappa Z_A+o(1)Z_B.\n\\]\nAngular spatial derivatives stay in divergence form and need only boundedness. Small relative changes preserve these bounds. Fix the mass first, then a bulk threshold \\(B_0\\), so all finitely many same-level rates are at most \\(2\\kappa+\\eta_0\\) in \\(\\min(t,v,-u)\\ge B_0\\); elsewhere a common bound is \\(C_0\\).</p><p>On the known support, nonbulk points occur only at times in\n\\[\nJ_T=[0,B_0]\\cup\n\\left[\\frac{(1-\\nu_0)T-\\omega_T-B_0}{2},L_T\\right],\n\\]\nwhose length is at most \\(\\nu_0T/2+O(B_0+1)\\). Choose \\(\\nu_0\\) after \\(C_0\\), and set\n\\[\nr_T=2\\kappa+\\eta_0+C_0\\mathbf1_{J_T},\n\\qquad R_T(t)=\\int_0^tr_T.\n\\]\nWith strict choices,\n\\[\nR_T(L_T)\\le(\\kappa+\\eta)T.\n\\]\nThis one nonnegative pointwise majorant works for both actual equations and the background equation at every used order. Remaining couplings strictly lower ordinary order and have subexponential integrals, so Lemma 10.1 applies.</p><p>With source \\(A^2e^{\\eta T}\\), entry bounds, and \\(K=60\\), it yields at every time\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\le60}E_j(t)\\le A^2e^{R_T(t)+\\eta_1T}.\n\\]\nIts terminal exponent is \\(-3\\kappa/4+\\eta+\\eta_1\\), improving \\(-5\\kappa/8\\) when the losses are small. The mass embedding gives the individual \\(C^{30}\\)-bound.</p><p>For each fixed \\(T\\), the lapse has positive minimum on the finite enlarged region. These mixed energies give ordinary local Cauchy norms with constants allowed to depend on \\(T\\). Lorentzian and temporal nondegeneracy and strict side margins persist. Restart on enlarged slice intervals and restrict by dependence; auxiliary reduced fields may be extended across sides without imposing constraints outside the retained domain. Local continuation rules out finite breakdown. Higher smooth norms satisfy differentiated linear bounds on this fixed slab. Bianchi and the exact entry constraints give zero gauge value and normal derivative, hence vacuum throughout.</p><p>Now regard both individual solutions as known and put \\(\\mathcal N(H)=\\mathfrak R(H)-\\mathcal LH\\). Taylor expansion in finite jets gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal N(H)=\\mathcal B_2(H,H)+\\mathcal N_{\\ge3}(H),\n\\]\nwith total derivative count at most two. Their difference satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathcal LF_T^{\\mathrm{odd}}\n=-2\\mathcal Lq_T\n-\\mathcal N(q_T+F_T^+)\n+\\mathcal N(-q_T+F_T^-).\n\\]\nPure packet quadratic terms cancel. Remaining quadratic terms contain packet–error or error–error factors; cubic remainders have pure size \\(A^3\\), with other terms absorbed by relative smallness.</p><p>Estimate through mixed order \\(10\\). Source derivatives have order at most \\(12\\), paid by individual mass level \\(12\\), not a degenerate angular derivative. Supremum factors use levels through \\(14\\), strictly below \\(60\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\|\\mathcal LF_T^{\\mathrm{odd}}(t)\\|_{H^{10}_{\\mathrm{mix}}}\n\\le e^{\\eta_2T+o(T)}\n\\left(A^3e^{R_T(t)}+A^4e^{2R_T(t)}\\right).\n\\]\nThe negligible \\(\\mathcal Lq_T\\) is absorbed because \\(-4\\kappa&lt;-3\\alpha\\). No lapse conversion is used here. Initial odd energies are cubic. Apply Lemma 10.1 with the same rate:\n\\[\n\\int_0^te^{R_T(t)-R_T(s)}A^3e^{R_T(s)}\\,ds\n=tA^3e^{R_T(t)},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\int_0^te^{R_T(t)-R_T(s)}A^4e^{2R_T(s)}\\,ds\n\\le tA^4e^{2R_T(t)}.\n\\]\nThe preserved intermediate-time difference prevents an additional terminal growth loss. Mass embedding gives the claimed odd bound.</p><p>Finally fix individual order \\(60\\), odd order \\(10\\), output \\(30\\), the prescribed data order, and all finite buffers. Allocate total losses below \\(\\delta&lt;\\kappa/32\\); choose mass, bulk threshold, then \\(\\nu_0\\), and exterior choices in their required order. Choose \\(\\zeta\\) only after positive frequency powers are fixed, and increase formal inverse accuracy afterwards.</p><p>The three error exponents are\n\\[\n-3\\kappa/4+\\delta,\\qquad\n-13\\kappa/8+\\delta,\\qquad\n-3\\kappa/2+\\delta.\n\\]\nThe packet \\(C^{30}\\)-exponent can be at most \\(-7\\kappa/8+\\kappa/32\\). Both total changes may therefore be bounded by \\(e^{-11\\kappa T/16}\\), giving \\(c=3\\kappa/16\\) after enlarging \\(T\\). Dividing odd bounds by \\(A\\), and total-change squares by \\(A\\), leaves strictly negative exponents, stable under every fixed-order \\(e^{o(T)}\\) factor. This proves all assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is \\(P_T=e^{o(T)}\\), uniform for all \\(0\\le s\\le t\\le L\\), such that \\( \\sum_{j\\le K}E_j(t) \\le P_T\\left[ e^{R_T(t)-R_T(s)}\\sum_{j\\le K}E_j(s) +\\int_s^te^{R_T(t)-R_T(\\tau)} \\sum_{j\\le K}S_j(\\tau)\\,d\\tau \\right]. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix finitely many levels \\(0,\\ldots,K\\), a time interval \\([0,L]\\), and nonnegative quantities satisfying\n  \\[\n  E_j'\\le r_TE_j+\\sum_{\\ell&lt;j}a_{j\\ell}(t)E_\\ell+S_j,\n  \\]\n  where \\(r_T,a_{j\\ell},S_j\\ge0\\),\n  \\[\n  R_T(t)=\\int_0^tr_T(s)\\,ds,\\qquad\n  \\int_0^La_{j\\ell}(t)\\,dt\\le e^{o(T)}.\n  \\]\n  Inequalities may be interpreted in integrated form.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(P_T=e^{o(T)}\\), uniform for all \\(0\\le s\\le t\\le L\\), such that\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\le K}E_j(t)\n\\le P_T\\left[\ne^{R_T(t)-R_T(s)}\\sum_{j\\le K}E_j(s)\n+\\int_s^te^{R_T(t)-R_T(\\tau)}\n\\sum_{j\\le K}S_j(\\tau)\\,d\\tau\n\\right].\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(A(t)=(a_{j\\ell}(t))\\), strictly lower triangular. Conjugate by \\(e^{-R_T(t)}\\) and compare with the nonnegative linear system. Its propagator is the finite time-ordered sum\n\\[\nU(t,s)=\\operatorname{Id}\n+\\sum_{m=1}^K\n\\int_{s&lt;\\tau_m&lt;\\cdots&lt;\\tau_1&lt;t}\nA(\\tau_1)\\cdots A(\\tau_m)\\,d\\tau_m\\cdots d\\tau_1.\n\\]\nAny product of \\(K+1\\) matrices vanishes because it requires \\(K+1\\) strict descents among \\(K+1\\) levels. No commutation assumption is needed. If \\(B_T=\\int_0^L\\|A(\\tau)\\|\\,d\\tau\\), then\n\\[\n\\|U(t,s)\\|\\le\\sum_{m=0}^K B_T^m/m!=e^{o(T)}.\n\\]\nFor any desired exponential tolerance, divide it among the finitely many factors. Variation of constants and removal of the common conjugation give the assertion, uniformly in both endpoints.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Proposition 7.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every sufficiently large finite \\(T\\), there is a smooth time-oriented vacuum development \\(\\mathcal N_T^\\pm\\) of the entire datum \\(d_T^\\pm\\), with the full \\(\\Sigma\\) Cauchy.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the complete corrected data \\(d_T^\\pm\\) from Proposition 9 and the finite interior metrics from Proposition 10.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix all construction and matching margins.</p><p class=\"li\">The available low interior estimate is\n  \\[\n  \\|H_T^\\pm\\|_{C^{30}_{\\mathrm{norm}}}\n  \\le\\epsilon_T,\\qquad\n  \\epsilon_T=C e^{-(\\kappa/2+c)T},\n  \\]\n  for some \\(c&gt;0\\). Norms mean ordinary derivatives of normalized arrays.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every sufficiently large finite \\(T\\), there is a smooth time-oriented vacuum development \\(\\mathcal N_T^\\pm\\) of the entire datum \\(d_T^\\pm\\), with the full \\(\\Sigma\\) Cauchy. It contains the finite observation and a fixed sufficiently small optical-coordinate box about \\(p_T\\), lying in \\(I^+_{\\mathcal N_T^\\pm}(\\Sigma)\\).</p><p>It admits an initial-data-preserving, time-orientation-preserving open isometric embedding into \\(\\mathcal M_{d_T^\\pm}\\). The entire physical constraint-correction tail is part of its initial data.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix one sign. First identify enlarged solution regions; choose retained portions afterwards. The inputs are the finite bridge lemma [20, Lemma 8.1], fixed-time wave-map identifications and collars [20, Proposition 8.2], and strict future cuts and joining order [20, Proposition 9.10].</p><p>The corrected data converge in \\(p_{10}\\), are smooth, and solve the constraints, so the finite bridge construction applies with the same depths, time bands, and past collar. Its artificial future faces are future-exiting. Compact pieces have finite-time compact-shadow control; ends have uniformly local symbol control and bounded causal speeds. No all-order smallness is needed.</p><p>The protected ball contains the fixed-origin ball of radius\n\\[\n(1-2\\beta)t_T-|C_e(t_T)|\n\\ge(1-2\\beta-s_*)t_T-|C_e(0)|\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nThus fixed compact shadows in lower joins are unchanged. Dependence identifies bounded central pieces, the bent compact part of the selected partial slice, and required unselected-end pieces with the background. The horizontal far part of \\(t_e=0\\), however, carries the actual corrected datum with its full tail, exactly as in the exterior evolution.</p><p>Choose a fixed matching time \\(t_a&gt;0\\) and enlarged band. Both lower and exterior solutions evolve the same geometric data on the partial slice. Solve the identifying wave map \\(\\Psi\\) from exterior to lower solution, with slice identification as value and matching future normal as normal derivative. On the fixed slab it remains a diffeomorphism on the smaller region.</p><p>Quantitatively, the source construction supplies metric \\(H^{10}\\), second-form \\(H^9\\), and corresponding uniformly local end control. The differentiated map equation at order ten uses Christoffel derivatives through nine. In a low-map and inverse-Jacobian bootstrap, tame estimates give\n\\[\nE_{10}(s)\\le C\\eta_T+C\\int_0^sE_{10}(\\sigma)\\,d\\sigma,\n\\qquad 0\\le s\\le t_a+\\delta_a,\\qquad \\eta_T\\to0,\n\\]\nwith constants independent of the growing cutoff. On ends use supremums of local-chart energies, not total volume. Uniqueness identifies chart overlaps. Gronwall and Sobolev embedding give \\(C^1\\) diffeomorphism control and \\(H^9\\) control after straightening. For global injectivity, compose with the inverse background identification: a uniformly \\(C^0,C^1\\)-small perturbation of identity can only identify nearby points, and in one enlarged convex chart integration of its derivative excludes a collision. Late \\(e^{o(T)}\\) high norms are not used as the top Gronwall coefficient.</p><p>Let \\(g_E,g_L\\) be the metrics and \\(h=\\Psi^*g_L\\). Holding \\(h\\) fixed as target, both \\(g_E\\) and \\(h\\) are vacuum metrics in the same reduced system. Matching slice values, future normals, and second forms gives equal spatial temporal derivatives. If \\(B_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_0(g_E-h)_{\\mu\\nu}\\), then \\(B_{ij}=0\\), and the lowered relative gauge components are\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_i=h^{00}B_{0i},\\qquad\n\\mathcal C_0=\\tfrac12h^{00}B_{00}.\n\\]\nThey vanish and \\(h^{00}\\ne0\\), so all first reduced jets agree. Local uniqueness proves actual isometry on the matching band. The small straightening is cut off farther back within the available margin. Compact joins use the unchanged background identification.</p><p>The exterior reaches the full needed entry interval and collar through \\(t_T+C_2\\), with outer and deeper margins. The entry conormal remains spacelike. The deep solution uses exactly the induced spacetime jets and the same target \\(g\\); reduced uniqueness therefore identifies actual entry collars. Approximate packet matching alone is not used for gluing.</p><p>Retain the full lower block, the needed exterior future, and the strict deep domain. With selected co-radius \\(r=|y|\\), choose the outer cutoff so that near its active boundary\n\\[\n\\frac d{dt_e}(r-R_{\\mathrm{out}}(t_e))\n\\ge V_{\\mathrm{out}}-V_{\\max}&gt;0\n\\]\non future causal curves. Near the matching level the retained set is\n\\[\n\\{t_e&lt;t_{\\mathrm{end}}\\}\n\\cap\\bigl(\\{t_e&lt;t_a\\}\\cup\\{r&lt;R_{\\mathrm{out}}(t_e)\\}\\bigr),\n\\]\nwith other active entry cuts imposed. Its lower member contains all radii. A past curve from the added future keeps a strictly negative \\(r-R_{\\mathrm{out}}\\) gap until reaching a genuine matching collar at \\(t_a\\), then the unrestricted lower block. It cannot have past endpoint at the outer/matching corner.</p><p>The deep retained inequalities are\n\\[\n0&lt;t&lt;L_T,\\qquad\nF_0=u+c_0t/T&lt;u_2,\\qquad\nF_1=v+c_0t/T&lt;T+c_2,\n\\]\nwith \\(t=0\\) attached. Relative cone control gives, for future causal vectors with \\(dt=1\\),\n\\[\ndu,dv\\ge-C\\epsilon_T,\\qquad\ndF_i\\ge c_0/T-C\\epsilon_T&gt;0.\n\\]\nThus a past curve preserves strict gaps from both omitted sides and from the top. Its limit at \\(t=0\\) lies strictly inside the actually supplied entry collar, not at an entry/side or entry/outer corner. Other active lower cuts have the source construction’s future-exit signs.</p><p>At intersections, retained prescriptions are finite unions or intersections of past-preserved strict sublevels. For unions retain the occupied member until entering a lower unrestricted member; for intersections retain every gap. For fixed \\(T\\), coordinate ranges are finite and the enlarged metric is smooth and nondegenerate, so temporal-coordinate causal speeds are bounded and finite endpoints have limits. The gap argument places limits in ordinary charts, real collars, or the lower block. Fixed-time end speeds exclude spatial escape to infinity.</p><p>Straighten joining collars as products, keep one lower and one upper side and only one copy of each face, and remove unused future faces and edges before quotienting. Faces are closed relative to retained pieces; identifications are homeomorphisms there and isometries on collars. The finite quotient is Hausdorff by the closed-face saturation argument, and collars give smooth charts. Overlapping joining prescriptions agree by uniqueness, so there is no duplicate continuation. The finite union of countable atlases is second countable.</p><p>Every past-inextendible causal curve above \\(\\Sigma\\) reaches the lower block and full bridge: it cannot stop at omitted faces, finite interior limits, or infinity in bounded time. Spacelike joining faces have one-way crossing orientation and cannot be revisited indefinitely. The lower block and its short past collar have the finite bridge Cauchy property. No causal curve crosses \\(\\Sigma\\) twice. Thus every inextendible causal curve meets \\(\\Sigma\\) exactly once.</p><p>The glued spacetime is a smooth globally hyperbolic vacuum development of the entire corrected datum. Only now apply the maximal-development embedding theorem [27, Definition 2.2 and Theorem 2.8]; equal-dimensional embeddings are open.</p><p>At \\(p_T\\), the time-top gap is at least \\(c_0\\), and side gaps are bounded below by\n\\[\nu_2-u_*-c_0/2-O(T^{-1}),\\qquad\nc_2-c_0/2-O(T^{-1}).\n\\]\nThey are fixed positive numbers. Bounded cut-function derivatives give a fixed sufficiently small coordinate box inside the retained region; its positive time above entry puts it in \\(I^+(\\Sigma)\\).</p><p>As in Remark 7.2, the attachment itself uses interior smallness only to retain temporal and strict outflow inequalities. The stronger \\(H=o(\\sqrt a)\\) estimate is used in the observer construction, not as an additional gluing hypothesis.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Proposition 7.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are seeds \\(w_T^\\pm\\to w\\) on \\(\\Sigma\\) whose future unit geodesics in \\(\\mathcal M_{d_T^\\pm}\\) reach \\(p_T\\) with tangent \\(U_T^\\pm\\), at finite positive proper times strictly below their full-development …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the branch seed \\(w\\) selected in Proposition 3 and its background geodesic \\(U=\\dot\\gamma_w\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the signed metrics and attachment of Propositions 10–11.</p><p class=\"li\">At the observation box define\n  \\[\n  S_T^\\pm=g^{-1}g_T^\\pm,\\qquad\n  I_T^\\pm=(S_T^\\pm)^{-1/2},\n  \\]\n  using the real analytic branch at identity. It is an isometry from \\(g\\) to \\(g_T^\\pm\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At \\(p_T\\), set \\(U_T^\\pm=I_T^\\pm U\\), and choose a smooth angular orthonormal frame \\(e_A\\), including the packet polarization direction at \\(p_T\\). Put\n  \\[\n  E_0^\\pm=I_T^\\pm L_0,\\qquad\n  E_1^\\pm=a^{-1}I_T^\\pm L_1,\\qquad\n  E_A^\\pm=I_T^\\pm e_A.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are seeds \\(w_T^\\pm\\to w\\) on \\(\\Sigma\\) whose future unit geodesics in \\(\\mathcal M_{d_T^\\pm}\\) reach \\(p_T\\) with tangent \\(U_T^\\pm\\), at finite positive proper times strictly below their full-development durations. Thus they lie in the original open seed set for large \\(T\\).</p><p>At \\(p_T\\), Euclidean component norms in the displayed frame and the transported positive test norms \\(k_{d_T^\\pm,w_T^\\pm}\\), and their induced operator norms, are uniformly equivalent independently of \\(T\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Because \\(S_T^\\pm\\) is \\(g\\)-self-adjoint, its analytic inverse square root commutes with it and is self-adjoint, so\n\\[\ng_T^\\pm(I_T^\\pm V,I_T^\\pm W)=g(V,W).\n\\]\nIt preserves the future orientation by continuity. Power-series differentiation in a constant-Gram normalized frame gives\n\\[\n\\|I_T^\\pm-\\operatorname{Id}\\|_{C^{30}_{\\mathrm{norm}}}\n+\\|(g_T^\\pm)^{-1}-g^{-1}\\|_{C^{30}_{\\mathrm{norm}}}\n\\le\\epsilon_Te^{o(T)}.\n\\]</p><p>At \\(p_T\\), the normalized components of \\(U\\) are\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{a_p}\\dot u,\\ p/\\sqrt{a_p},\\ \\Xi^{(A)}),\n\\]\nof size \\(O(a_p^{-1/2})\\). The new covector is\n\\[\n\\pi'=g_T^\\pm(I_T^\\pm U,\\cdot)\n=g(U,(I_T^\\pm)^{-1}\\cdot).\n\\]\nUsing \\(\\partial_u=\\sqrt a\\,\\widehat e_0-b^A\\partial_A\\), \\(\\partial_v=\\sqrt a\\,\\widehat e_1\\), one obtains\n\\[\n|Y_T^\\pm(T)-Y_0(T)|\n\\le C\\epsilon_T/\\sqrt{a_p}=o(1),\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\pi_v'-\\pi_v|\\le C\\epsilon_T,\\qquad\ndv(U_T^\\pm)=\\frac{p+O(\\epsilon_T)}{a_p}&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe new unit covector lies in the tube and root interval, so Lemma 12.2 identifies it with the future root. The \\(a_p^{-1/2}\\) loss is paid once at this terminal conversion.</p><p>Choose \\(v_0\\) and a smaller state tube with fixed boundary margin. The background track on \\(v_0\\le v\\le T\\) has fixed positive gaps\n\\[\nu_2-u(v)-c_0t(v)/T\n\\ge u_2-u_*-c_0/2-O(T^{-1}),\n\\]\n\\[\nT+c_2-v-c_0t(v)/T\n\\ge c_2-c_0/2-O(T^{-1}),\n\\]\n\\[\nL_T-t(v)\\ge c_0,\\qquad t(v)\\ge t(v_0)&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>Solve backward from the terminal state only while inside both the actual deep domain and the state tube, stopping no later than \\(v_0\\). Split\n\\[\nV_T^\\pm(v,Y_T^\\pm)-V_0(v,Y_0)\n=\n[V_T^\\pm(v,Y_T^\\pm)-V_0(v,Y_T^\\pm)]\n+[V_0(v,Y_T^\\pm)-V_0(v,Y_0)].\n\\]\nLemma 12.2 bounds the first by \\(C\\epsilon_T^{\\mathrm{sh}}\\); Lemma 12.1 bounds the second by \\(\\Lambda(v)|Y_T^\\pm-Y_0|\\). Backward Gronwall gives\n\\[\n\\sup_{v\\le s\\le T}|Y_T^\\pm(s)-Y_0(s)|\n\\le\n\\exp\\!\\left(\\int_{v_0}^\\infty\\Lambda\\right)\nC\\left(\\frac{\\epsilon_T}{\\sqrt{a_p}}\n+T\\epsilon_T^{\\mathrm{sh}}\\right)\n=:\\delta_T\\to0.\n\\]\nFor large \\(T\\) this excludes every state or spacetime boundary stop. At any finite interior endpoint the smooth finite-\\(T\\) ODE continues. Thus the trajectory reaches \\(v_0\\), without extending the perturbed field outside the constructed spacetime.</p><p>From \\(v_0\\) to \\(\\Sigma\\), choose a compact background flow neighborhood with strict collar margins. Its bounded-\\(v\\) deep portion is below the changed support for large \\(T\\), and lower shadows are protected. Exact earlier agreement and the attachment identify this neighborhood with the background. Transverse crossings of \\(S\\) and \\(\\Sigma\\), and smooth finite-time flow dependence, produce seeds \\(w_T^\\pm\\to w\\).</p><p>The resulting path is a future unit geodesic. Its late proper duration is finite because\n\\[\nd\\tau/dv=2a/\\partial_zF_T;\n\\]\nthe earlier compact duration is finite. The observation box gives positive further local continuation at \\(p_T\\). Proposition 11 embeds it into the full development, so observation occurs strictly before maximal duration.</p><p>The displayed \\(E^\\pm\\)-frame has constant Lorentz Gram matrix\n\\[\ng_T^\\pm(E_0^\\pm,E_1^\\pm)=-1,\\qquad\ng_T^\\pm(E_A^\\pm,E_B^\\pm)=\\delta_{AB}.\n\\]\nBy isometry, components of \\(U_T^\\pm\\) are \\((\\dot u,p,\\Xi^{(A)})\\), in a compact subset of the future unit hyperboloid. Hence\n\\[\nr_{U_T^\\pm}\n=g_T^\\pm+2g_T^\\pm(\\cdot,U_T^\\pm)\\otimes\ng_T^\\pm(\\cdot,U_T^\\pm)\n\\]\nis uniformly equivalent to Euclidean component norm. This form and the test form are parallel along the observer; their initial comparison is uniform by \\(d_T^\\pm\\to d\\), \\(w_T^\\pm\\to w\\), and Lemma 2.1. Parallel transport preserves comparison constants and therefore induced operator-norm comparison. Gram–Schmidt followed by a constant rotation provides the required angular frame with fixed jets \\(e^{o(T)}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are a fixed late \\(v_0\\) and a fixed bounded open canonical state set \\(K\\), containing the late track with fixed positive boundary margin, such that positions stay in one angular chart and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the background branch of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">In canonical coordinates write\n  \\[\n  \\pi=\\pi_u\\,du+z\\,dv+P_A\\,d\\theta^A,\\qquad\n  p=-(\\pi_u+b^AP_A).\n  \\]\n  With \\(v\\) as time, the background unit shell and Hamiltonian are\n  \\[\n  2pz+a(1+\\gamma^{AB}P_AP_B)=0,\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathcal H_0=-z_0=\\frac{a(1+\\gamma^{AB}P_AP_B)}{2p}.\n  \\]\n  Let \\(Y=(u,\\theta,\\pi_u,P)\\) be the canonical state.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are a fixed late \\(v_0\\) and a fixed bounded open canonical state set \\(K\\), containing the late track with fixed positive boundary margin, such that positions stay in one angular chart and\n\\[\np(v,Y)\\ge p_*&gt;0\\qquad(v\\ge v_0,\\ Y\\in K).\n\\]\nOn a slightly enlarged set the background field \\(V_0\\) satisfies\n\\[\n|V_0(v,Y)|+|D_YV_0(v,Y)|\n\\le Ce^{-\\kappa v}e^{o(v)}=:\\Lambda(v),\n\\qquad\n\\int_{v_0}^\\infty\\Lambda(v)\\,dv&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nHere \\(e^{o(v)}\\) uses the current endpoint \\(v\\), not terminal \\(T\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 5 bounds \\(u,P,p\\), hence \\(\\pi_u\\). Choose \\(v_0\\) with the angular track in an inner chart about its limit. Bounded first derivatives of \\(b\\), on fixed bounded momentum ranges, give\n\\[\n|p(Y)-p(Y_0(v))|\\le C|Y-Y_0(v)|\n\\]\nwith \\(C\\) independent of \\(v\\). Choose a fixed radius \\(r_0\\) below the angular margin and \\(\\inf p(Y_0(v))/(2C)\\). Then \\(p\\) remains uniformly positive on those pointwise neighborhoods, without multiplying \\(C\\) by interval length.</p><p>Twice differentiating \\(\\mathcal H_0\\) uses finitely many ordinary background jets and bounded inverse powers of \\(p\\). Every derivative of \\(a\\) retains \\(a\\), and other jets cost \\(e^{o(v)}\\), so the field and its first state derivative retain \\(e^{-\\kappa v}\\). The resulting integrable bound gives a state limit \\(Y_\\infty\\). Increase \\(v_0\\) so the entire track lies within \\(r_0/8\\) of this limit. A ball of radius \\(r_0/4\\) about \\(Y_\\infty\\), enlarged to radius \\(r_0/2\\), lies in the previously controlled neighborhoods and supplies the fixed set.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.2", "orig": "Lemma 7.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The new unit shell has a simple root \\(z=z_T^\\pm(v,Y)\\) with \\( \\|z_T^\\pm-z_0\\|_{C_Y^2}\\le\\epsilon_T^{\\mathrm{sh}}, \\qquad \\epsilon_T^{\\mathrm{sh}}=\\epsilon_Te^{o(T)}. \\) Its canonical field obeys …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed state tube of Lemma 12.1, intersected with the actual deep domain.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(g'=g_T^\\pm\\), with relative \\(C^{30}\\)-size \\(\\epsilon_T\\le Ce^{-(\\kappa/2+c)T}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The new unit shell has a simple root \\(z=z_T^\\pm(v,Y)\\) with\n\\[\n\\|z_T^\\pm-z_0\\|_{C_Y^2}\\le\\epsilon_T^{\\mathrm{sh}},\n\\qquad\n\\epsilon_T^{\\mathrm{sh}}=\\epsilon_Te^{o(T)}.\n\\]\nIts canonical field obeys \\(|V_T^\\pm-V_0|\\le C\\epsilon_T^{\\mathrm{sh}}\\). The root is future pointing and \\(dv/d\\tau&gt;0\\). These are absolute estimates for \\(\\pi_v\\), not relative estimates for \\(\\pi_v/a\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>In a background angular orthonormal frame, let \\(Q_{\\alpha\\beta}\\) denote inverse-metric-difference coefficients in\n\\[\n(a^{-1/2}L_0,a^{-1/2}L_1,e_A).\n\\]\nTheir needed jets are \\(O(\\epsilon_Te^{o(T)})\\), from analytic inversion. Multiplying the new shell by \\(a\\) gives\n\\[\nF_T(v,Y,z)=2pz+a(1+\\gamma^{AB}P_AP_B)+\\Delta_T(v,Y,z)=0,\n\\]\nwhere exactly\n\\[\n\\Delta_T\n=Q_{00}p^2-2Q_{01}pz+Q_{11}z^2\n-2\\sqrt a\\,Q_{0A}pP_{(A)}\n+2\\sqrt a\\,Q_{1A}zP_{(A)}\n+aQ_{AB}P_{(A)}P_{(B)}.\n\\]\nAll ordinary and state derivatives through three on bounded states and \\(z\\)-intervals are \\(O(\\epsilon_Te^{o(T)})\\). Frame differentiation creates no additional inverse lapse; null covector factors have already been canceled by the external \\(a\\).</p><p>Choose a bounded \\(z\\)-interval containing \\(z_0\\) with fixed margin. Then\n\\[\n\\partial_zF_T=2p+\\partial_z\\Delta_T\\ge p_*&gt;0.\n\\]\nValues at \\(z_0\\pm C\\epsilon_T^{\\mathrm{sh}}\\) have opposite signs. The unique nearby root has the displayed \\(C_Y^2\\)-bound by implicit differentiation. The reduced Hamiltonian is \\(-z_T^\\pm\\), giving the field difference. A nonzero quadratic coefficient in \\(z\\) is permitted.</p><p>For the affine Hamiltonian\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{aff}}\n=\\tfrac12(g'^{-1}(\\pi,\\pi)+1),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\partial_zF_T=2a\\,\\partial_z\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{aff}}\n=2a\\,dv/d\\tau&gt;0.\n\\]\nSince \\(t=(u+v)/2\\) remains temporal and \\(\\partial_{\\pi_u}p=-1\\),\n\\[\n4a\\,dt/d\\tau\n=\\partial_{\\pi_u}F_T+\\partial_zF_T\n=2p-2z+\\partial_{\\pi_u}\\Delta_T+\\partial_z\\Delta_T&gt;0.\n\\]\nHere \\(z_0\\le0\\) and absolute root closeness controls errors. Thus the root is future oriented. Dividing the affine canonical equations by \\(dv/d\\tau\\) yields the canonical equations of \\(-z_T^\\pm\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Proposition 8.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every sufficiently large \\(T\\), at least one sign has \\( |C_T^\\pm|>A_T. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the signed observations, adaptive frames, and fixed coordinate box of Propositions 10–12.</p><p class=\"li\">On the box, \\(a/a_p\\) and \\(a_p/a\\) are bounded, where\n  \\[\n  a_p=a(p_T)\\asymp e^{-\\kappa T},\\qquad\n  A_T=e^{-7\\kappa T/8},\\qquad \\lambda_T=e^{\\zeta T}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose an angular orthonormal frame with \\(e_2(p_T)=X_T\\), the packet polarization direction, and define\n  \\[\n  I_T^\\pm=(g^{-1}g_T^\\pm)^{-1/2},\\quad\n  E_0^\\pm=I_T^\\pm L_0,\\quad\n  E_1^\\pm=a^{-1}I_T^\\pm L_1,\\quad\n  E_A^\\pm=I_T^\\pm e_A.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define the scaled sectional observations\n  \\[\n  C_T^\\pm\n  =a_p^2\\operatorname{Riem}(g_T^\\pm)\n  (E_1^\\pm,E_2^\\pm,E_1^\\pm,E_2^\\pm).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every sufficiently large \\(T\\), at least one sign has\n\\[\n|C_T^\\pm|&gt;A_T.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The principal second-derivative curvature variation on physical slots \\(L_1,X_T,L_1,X_T\\), up to curvature convention, is\n\\[\n\\tfrac12(\\nabla_1\\nabla_1h_{XX}\n+\\nabla_X\\nabla_Xh_{11}\n-2\\nabla_1\\nabla_Xh_{1X}).\n\\]\nFor\n\\[\nh_T^p=A_T\\operatorname{tr.rev.}_g\n\\operatorname{Re}\\left(e^{i\\lambda_Tv}\n\\sum_{j=0}^J\\lambda_T^{-j}p_j\\right),\n\\]\nonly two phase derivatives on the \\(XX\\)-entry contribute order \\(A_T\\lambda_T^2\\), since \\(dv(L_1)=1\\), \\(dv(X_T)=0\\). Leading trace reversal has no effect. Derivatives on amplitudes or connections, \\(j\\ge1\\) terms, and algebraic isometry-slot variations on scaled background curvature are at most \\(A_T\\lambda_Te^{o(T)}\\). Therefore\n\\[\nD\\mathcal C_T(0)[q_T]\n=\\pm\\tfrac12A_T\\lambda_T^2\n\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(e^{i\\lambda_TT}p_0(X_T,X_T)\\bigr)\n+O(A_T\\lambda_Te^{o(T)}).\n\\]\nThe phase and direction chosen in Proposition 8 make the real contraction positive with it and its reciprocal \\(e^{o(T)}\\). An observer-orthogonal screen representative differs by an \\(L_0\\)-multiple and has the same contraction because \\(p_0(L_0,\\cdot)=0\\).</p><p>Since \\(\\lambda_T=e^{\\zeta T}\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{A_T\\lambda_Te^{o(T)}}{A_T\\lambda_T^2\\sigma_T}\n=e^{-\\zeta T+o(T)}\\to0,\\qquad\n\\lambda_T^2\\sigma_T\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nLemma 13.1 gives \\(|C_T^+-C_T^-|&gt;2A_T\\), so at least one sign exceeds \\(A_T\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a smooth function \\(\\mathcal C_T\\) of the relative metric array and its ordinary jets through two such that \\( C_T^\\pm=\\mathcal C_T(H_T^\\pm). \\) On a fixed small relative-array neighborhood its first two …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 13’s box and frames.</p><p class=\"li\">Relative arrays satisfy\n  \\[\n  \\|H_T^\\pm\\|_{C^{30}}\\le e^{-(\\kappa/2+c)T},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  e^{o(T)}\n  \\left(\\|F_T^{\\mathrm{odd}}\\|_{C^2}\n  +\\|H_T^+\\|_{C^2}^2+\\|H_T^-\\|_{C^2}^2\\right)=o(A_T).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a smooth function \\(\\mathcal C_T\\) of the relative metric array and its ordinary jets through two such that\n\\[\nC_T^\\pm=\\mathcal C_T(H_T^\\pm).\n\\]\nOn a fixed small relative-array neighborhood its first two derivatives have norms at most \\(e^{o(T)}\\). Consequently\n\\[\nC_T^+-C_T^-\n=2D\\mathcal C_T(0)[q_T]+o(A_T).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use a normalized angular-orthonormal frame with constant Gram matrix \\(\\eta\\), put \\(Z_\\alpha=\\sqrt a\\,\\widehat e_\\alpha\\), and let \\(V_\\alpha\\) be its background connection matrices. Proposition 7 bounds their jets, bracket coefficients in the \\(Z\\)-frame, and normalized \\(a\\operatorname{Riem}(g)\\) by \\(e^{o(T)}\\).</p><p>For a relative change \\(H\\), write \\(M=\\eta+H\\), with physical tensor \\(k\\), and \\(K=\\nabla^{g+k}-\\nabla^g\\). Set\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_\\alpha H_{\\beta\\delta}\n=Z_\\alpha H_{\\beta\\delta}\n-(V_\\alpha)^\\rho{}_\\beta H_{\\rho\\delta}\n-(V_\\alpha)^\\rho{}_\\delta H_{\\beta\\rho}.\n\\]\nThe array of \\(\\sqrt a\\,K\\) is\n\\[\nW^\\mu{}_{\\alpha\\beta}\n=\\tfrac12(M^{-1})^{\\mu\\delta}\n(\\mathcal D_\\alpha H_{\\beta\\delta}\n+\\mathcal D_\\beta H_{\\alpha\\delta}\n-\\mathcal D_\\delta H_{\\alpha\\beta}).\n\\]\nThus \\(V+W\\) is the new connection along \\(Z\\) in the unchanged normalized frame. If\n\\([Z_\\alpha,Z_\\beta]=b^\\rho{}_{\\alpha\\beta}Z_\\rho\\), then\n\\[\nR^{g+k}(Z_\\alpha,Z_\\beta)\n=Z_\\alpha(V_\\beta+W_\\beta)-Z_\\beta(V_\\alpha+W_\\alpha)\n+[V_\\alpha+W_\\alpha,V_\\beta+W_\\beta]\n-b^\\rho{}_{\\alpha\\beta}(V_\\rho+W_\\rho).\n\\]\nThese are smooth finite-jet expressions with subexponential coefficients. Contracting with \\(M\\) gives normalized covariant entries of \\(a\\operatorname{Riem}(g+k)\\); including the analytic isometry \\(I(H)\\) preserves bounds. At \\(p_T\\), the sectional quantity is \\(a_p\\) times curvature on slots \\(I(H)\\widehat e_1,I(H)e_2,I(H)\\widehat e_1,I(H)e_2\\), proving the scaled smoothness assertion.</p><p>Taylor expansion separately at both signs gives\n\\[\nC_T^+-C_T^-\n=2D\\mathcal C_T(0)[q_T]\n+D\\mathcal C_T(0)[F_T^{\\mathrm{odd}}]+\\mathcal E_T,\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\mathcal E_T|\n\\le e^{o(T)}(\\|H_T^+\\|_{C^2}^2+\\|H_T^-\\|_{C^2}^2).\n\\]\nThe assumed estimates make the last two terms \\(o(A_T)\\). The background value cancels exactly. This is a scaled coefficient estimate, not a bounded observer-frame curvature assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Lemma 8.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For sufficiently large \\(T\\), all these loops exist inside the attached future observation box.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Choose a sign supplied by Proposition 13 and abbreviate its metric, isometry, and frame by \\(g',I,E\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Hold \\(a_p\\) constant on the observation box and set\n  \\[\n  f_\\alpha=a_pE_\\alpha,\\qquad\n  \\nabla^{g'}_{f_\\alpha}E_\\beta\n  =G^\\mu{}_{\\alpha\\beta}E_\\mu.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(h_T=e^{-\\kappa T/16}\\). For \\(0\\le h\\le17h_T\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\Psi_h(s,t)=\\operatorname{Fl}^1_{h(sf_1+tf_2)}(p_T),\n  \\qquad (s,t)\\in[0,1]^2.\n  \\]\n  This is the actual time-one flow of the single parameter-dependent field.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\ell_h\\) be the image of the positively oriented square boundary, based at \\(p_T=\\Psi_h(0,0)\\). Let \\(\\mathcal H_T(h)\\) be its transport matrix in \\(E(p_T)\\), and \\(\\mathcal L_T(h)\\) its developed length in the transported test metric.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large \\(T\\), all these loops exist inside the attached future observation box. There is \\(Q_T=e^{o(T)}\\) with\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_T(h)\\le a_phQ_T,\\qquad\n\\sup_{0\\le h\\le17h_T}\\|\\mathcal H_T^{(18)}(h)\\|\\le Q_T.\n\\]\nMoreover\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_T(0)=\\operatorname{Id},\\qquad\n\\tfrac12\\mathcal H_T''(0)\n=\\pm a_p^2R^{g'}(E_1,E_2)(p_T)\n\\]\nas based endomorphism matrices.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use flow variable \\(r\\in[0,1]\\), and set \\(c^\\alpha=h(s\\delta_1^\\alpha+t\\delta_2^\\alpha)\\). The flow solves\n\\[\n\\partial_r\\Psi=c^\\alpha f_\\alpha(\\Psi),\\qquad \\Psi(0)=p_T.\n\\]\nBy Lemma 14.1, speed and position-Lipschitz coefficient are at most \\(hQ_T\\). Since \\(17h_TQ_T\\to0\\), the flow stays in the fixed strict box through time one.</p><p>Parameter derivatives in \\((h,s,t)\\) satisfy linear equations with order-current homogeneous coefficient \\(c^\\alpha Df_\\alpha(\\Psi)=O(hQ_T)\\) and finite products of lower variations and coefficient derivatives as sources. Their initial values vanish. Induction through total order nineteen gives subexponential bounds; \\(\\exp(O(hQ_T))\\le2\\) for large \\(T\\).</p><p>To retain physical length \\(a_p\\), solve for the \\(s\\)-variation in the \\(f\\)-frame:\n\\[\n\\frac{dJ_s^\\gamma}{dr}\n=h\\delta_1^\\gamma\n+c^\\alpha J_s^\\beta B^\\gamma{}_{\\beta\\alpha}(\\Psi),\n\\qquad J_s(0)=0.\n\\]\nThen \\(J_s^\\beta f_\\beta(\\Psi)=\\partial_s\\Psi\\): differentiating and using brackets gives exactly the coordinate variation equation, with zero initial value. The \\(t\\)-equation has source \\(h\\delta_2^\\gamma\\). Thus\n\\[\n|J_s|+|J_t|\\le hQ_T.\n\\]\nNo inverse coordinate frame is estimated.</p><p>Differentiate these equations directly in \\(h\\), including at \\(h=0\\). The current-order coefficient remains \\(O(hQ_T)\\); sources use lower \\(J\\)-derivatives, known coordinate variations, and fixed bracket jets. Induction gives\n\\[\n\\sup|\\partial_h^nJ_s|+\\sup|\\partial_h^nJ_t|\\le e^{o(T)},\n\\qquad 1\\le n\\le18.\n\\]\nNo division by \\(h\\) occurs.</p><p>Each oriented edge tangent is \\(J^\\alpha f_\\alpha\\), with \\(J\\) one of the signed variations. Parallel transport in the moving \\(E\\)-frame satisfies\n\\[\n\\frac{dP}{dz}=-\\mathcal G(z,h)P,\\qquad\n\\mathcal G^\\mu{}_\\beta\n=G^\\mu{}_{\\alpha\\beta}(\\Psi_h)J^\\alpha.\n\\]\nThe coefficient is \\(O(hQ_T)\\), and its first eighteen \\(h\\)-derivatives are subexponential. Fundamental matrices and inverses are bounded by \\(\\exp(O(hQ_T))\\). Differentiating this ODE eighteen times and multiplying the four edge matrices proves the transport-derivative bound.</p><p>The edge tangent has \\(E\\)-components \\(a_pJ\\). Its transported-positive norm is the base norm after \\(P^{-1}\\), at most\n\\[\nCa_p|P^{-1}J|\\le a_phQ_T\n\\]\nby Proposition 12 and the variation bound. Sum four edge intervals to obtain the actual developed-length estimate.</p><p>For the quadratic coefficient, consider\n\\[\n\\Phi(\\sigma,\\vartheta)\n=\\operatorname{Fl}^1_{\\sigma f_1+\\vartheta f_2}(p_T).\n\\]\nThe loop is the image of \\(\\partial[0,h]^2\\). For each fixed \\(T\\), pull back the connection and choose a smooth frame equal to \\(E(p_T)\\) at the origin with zero connection one-form there. Its connection is \\(O(|\\sigma|+|\\vartheta|)\\); products of two edge integrals are \\(O(h^4)\\). Stokes’ formula gives the \\(h^2\\)-coefficient as curvature at the origin, up to sign. Since \\(d\\Phi_0\\) sends coordinate vectors to \\(f_1,f_2\\), this is \\(\\pm a_p^2R^{g'}(E_1,E_2)\\). Uniform derivative estimates were obtained independently in the original frame.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Coordinate components of \\(f_\\alpha\\), connection arrays \\(G^\\mu{}_{\\alpha\\beta}\\), and bracket coefficients \\(B^\\mu{}_{\\alpha\\beta}\\) have ordinary jets through order twenty bounded by \\(e^{o(T)}\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 14’s sign, frames, and \\(f_\\alpha\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  [f_\\alpha,f_\\beta]=B^\\mu{}_{\\alpha\\beta}f_\\mu.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Coordinate components of \\(f_\\alpha\\), connection arrays \\(G^\\mu{}_{\\alpha\\beta}\\), and bracket coefficients \\(B^\\mu{}_{\\alpha\\beta}\\) have ordinary jets through order twenty bounded by \\(e^{o(T)}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(\\widetilde e_\\alpha=I\\widehat e_\\alpha\\), and define\n\\[\n\\nabla^{g'}_{\\sqrt a\\,\\widetilde e_\\alpha}\\widetilde e_\\beta\n=\\widetilde V^\\mu{}_{\\alpha\\beta}\\widetilde e_\\mu.\n\\]\nThe connection-difference and frame-change formulas, with the \\(C^{30}\\)-bound, give\n\\[\n\\|\\widetilde V-V\\|_{C^{20}}\n+\\|I-\\operatorname{Id}\\|_{C^{21}}\n\\le e^{-(\\kappa/2+c_1)T}\n\\]\nfor some \\(c_1&gt;0\\).</p><p>Let \\(s_0=1/2,s_1=-1/2,s_2=s_3=0\\), so \\(E_\\alpha=a^{s_\\alpha}\\widetilde e_\\alpha\\). The exact conversion is\n\\[\nG^\\mu{}_{\\alpha\\beta}\n=\\frac{a_p}{a}\\,\na^{1/2+s_\\alpha+s_\\beta-s_\\mu}\n\\widetilde V^\\mu{}_{\\alpha\\beta}\n+s_\\beta(f_\\alpha\\log a)\\delta^\\mu_\\beta.\n\\]\nPrecisely seven individual entries have a negative displayed power:\n\\[\n(1,1,0): -1;\\quad\n(A,1,0): -1/2;\\quad\n(1,1,A): -1/2;\\quad\n(1,A,0): -1/2,\n\\quad A=2,3.\n\\]\nExhaustiveness follows by separating \\(\\mu=1\\), angular \\(\\mu\\), and \\(\\mu=0\\).</p><p>Null metric compatibility gives\n\\[\n\\widetilde V^0{}_{\\alpha1}=0\n\\]\nidentically, controlling the first three entries and all their derivatives. For the remaining four, the background identities\n\\[\n\\nabla^g_{L_1}L_1=(L_1\\log a)L_1,\\qquad\ng(\\widehat e_1,e_A)=0\n\\]\ngive \\(V^A{}_{11}=V^0{}_{1A}=0\\), including their derivatives. Thus their perturbed values are exponentially small, and their worst multiplier \\(a^{-1/2}\\) leaves \\(O(e^{-c_1T})\\). Derivatives of boost factors retain the same powers.</p><p>A normalized radial basis vector has coordinate size \\(a^{-1/2}e^{o(T)}\\), an angular one \\(e^{o(T)}\\). The worst coordinate multipliers for \\(f_\\alpha\\) are \\(a_pa^{s_\\alpha-1/2}\\) for radial output and \\(a_pa^{s_\\alpha}\\) for angular output. Since \\(a\\asymp a_p\\), their worst powers are \\(a^0\\) and \\(a^{1/2}\\). Hence all twenty coordinate jets of \\(f\\), and \\(f_\\alpha\\log a\\), are subexponential. Nonnegative-power connection entries are then controlled.</p><p>Finally torsion-freeness and constant \\(a_p\\) give\n\\[\n[f_\\alpha,f_\\beta]\n=(G^\\mu{}_{\\alpha\\beta}-G^\\mu{}_{\\beta\\alpha})f_\\mu.\n\\]\nThere is no \\(a_p^{-1}\\) factor and no need to estimate the inverse coordinate frame.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Lemma 8.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The exact identity \\( \\frac{F''(0)}2 =h^{-2}\\sum_{j=0}^{17}c_jF(jh) -\\frac{h^{-2}}{17!}\\sum_{j=0}^{17}c_j \\int_0^{jh}(jh-t)^{17}F^{(18)}(t)\\,dt \\) holds with Bochner integrals.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V\\) be a finite-dimensional normed vector space, \\(h&gt;0\\), and \\(F:[0,17h]\\to V\\) be \\(C^{18}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  S_1=\\sum_{r=1}^{17}\\frac1r,\\qquad\n  S_2=\\sum_{r=1}^{17}\\frac1{r^2},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  c_0=\\frac{S_1^2-S_2}{2},\\qquad\n  c_j=\\frac{(-1)^j}{j}\\binom{17}{j}\n  \\left(S_1-\\frac1j\\right),\\quad1\\le j\\le17.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The exact identity\n\\[\n\\frac{F''(0)}2\n=h^{-2}\\sum_{j=0}^{17}c_jF(jh)\n-\\frac{h^{-2}}{17!}\\sum_{j=0}^{17}c_j\n\\int_0^{jh}(jh-t)^{17}F^{(18)}(t)\\,dt\n\\]\nholds with Bochner integrals.</p><p>If \\(F(0)=0\\), \\(\\|F(jh)\\|\\le bjh\\), and \\(\\sup\\|F^{(18)}\\|\\le M\\), then\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\frac{F''(0)}2\\right\\|\n\\le K_1\\frac bh+K_2Mh^{16},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nK_1=\\sum_{j=1}^{17}|c_j|j,\\qquad\nK_2=\\frac1{18!}\\sum_{j=1}^{17}|c_j|j^{18}.\n\\]\nThese constants are finite and independent of \\(F,h,T\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let\n\\[\nL_j(x)=\\prod_{\\substack{0\\le r\\le17\\\\r\\ne j}}\\frac{x-r}{j-r}.\n\\]\nThe coefficient of \\(x^2\\) in \\(L_0\\) is\n\\[\n\\sum_{1\\le r&lt;s\\le17}(rs)^{-1}=c_0.\n\\]\nFor \\(j\\ge1\\), \\(L_j\\) has a factor \\(x\\),\n\\[\nL_j'(0)=(-1)^{j-1}\\binom{17}{j}/j,\n\\]\nand the logarithmic derivative at zero of the remaining product is \\(-S_1+1/j\\). Thus its \\(x^2\\)-coefficient is \\(c_j\\). Lagrange interpolation gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=0}^{17}c_jj^k=\n\\begin{cases}\n1,&amp;k=2,\\\\\n0,&amp;0\\le k\\le17,\\ k\\ne2.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nApply these identities to the Taylor polynomial\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=0}^{17}F^{(k)}(0)t^k/k!\n\\]\nat \\(t=jh\\), and insert Taylor’s integral remainder. This gives the exact formula. Bound each remainder by \\(M(jh)^{18}/18!\\) and the values by \\(bjh\\) to obtain the inequality.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "Proposition 8.6", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The signed data may be chosen with \\( p_m(d_T^\\pm-d)\\to0, \\) and, for every sufficiently large \\(T\\), at least one of \\(d_T^+,d_T^-\\) does not belong to \\(\\mathcal A(O,B)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a passing test \\(\\mathcal A(O,B)\\), a datum \\(d\\in\\mathcal A(O,B)\\), and a branch seed \\(w\\in O\\) selected by Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix any finite data order \\(m\\ge10\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the signed constructions of Propositions 8–14 with the finite choices required there.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The signed data may be chosen with\n\\[\np_m(d_T^\\pm-d)\\to0,\n\\]\nand, for every sufficiently large \\(T\\), at least one of \\(d_T^+,d_T^-\\) does not belong to \\(\\mathcal A(O,B)\\).</p><p>Thus each passing set has empty relative interior in \\(\\mathcal U\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose the sign from Proposition 13. Proposition 12 supplies an adaptive seed \\(w_T\\in O\\) reaching \\(p_T\\) at positive proper time strictly before its full-development endpoint. Proposition 11 places every loop of Proposition 14 in the future of the entire initial surface in that full development. Their lengths tend uniformly to zero, so are strictly below \\(B^{-1}\\).</p><p>Suppose the selected datum still passes. The test inequality, uniform frame comparison, and Proposition 14 imply\n\\[\n\\|\\mathcal H_T(jh_T)-\\operatorname{Id}\\|\n\\le a_pjh_Te^{o(T)},\\qquad 0\\le j\\le17.\n\\]\nThe fixed \\(B\\) and comparison constants are included in \\(e^{o(T)}\\). Apply Proposition 15 to\n\\[\nF=\\mathcal H_T-\\operatorname{Id},\\qquad\nb=a_pe^{o(T)},\\qquad M=e^{o(T)}.\n\\]\nUsing the quadratic coefficient gives\n\\[\n\\|a_p^2R^{g'}(E_1,E_2)(p_T)\\|\n\\le e^{o(T)}(a_p/h_T+h_T^{16})\n\\]\n\\[\n\\le e^{o(T)}\n\\left(e^{-15\\kappa T/16}+e^{-\\kappa T}\\right)\n=o(A_T).\n\\]\nIndeed \\(A_T=e^{-14\\kappa T/16}\\), and both ratios have strictly negative exponential rates.</p><p>The constant Lorentz Gram matrix of \\(E\\) makes\n\\[\na_p^2\\operatorname{Riem}(g')(E_1,E_2,E_1,E_2)\n\\]\na fixed matrix contraction of this endomorphism. It is \\(o(A_T)\\), contradicting the selected \\(|C_T^\\pm|&gt;A_T\\). Hence the selected sign fails.</p><p>All quantifiers can be fixed in forward order. First choose the branch and strict observation/collar margins. Fix \\(m\\), individual energy order \\(60\\), odd order \\(10\\), ordinary metric order \\(30\\), and all finite constraint, exterior, gauge-normal-jet, trace, restriction, and loop buffers. Allocate a total exponential loss below \\(\\kappa/32\\). Fix mass, bulk thresholds, late-support fraction, exterior radius, and protected-ball tolerance in the order required by Propositions 9–10. Then choose \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\) for every fixed positive frequency power. Only afterwards increase finite transport, Taylor, and matching degrees to obtain inverse accuracy, for instance\n\\[\n7\\kappa/8+J\\zeta&gt;4\\kappa.\n\\]\nProposition 8 ensures this does not increase the positive output powers. All further coefficient orders remain finite; finally take \\(T\\) large to absorb their subexponential constants, attain prescribed \\(p_m\\)-proximity, and ensure \\(w_T^\\pm\\in O\\).</p><p>The three strict comparisons consumed are\n\\[\n\\|H_T^\\pm\\|_{C^{30}}=o(\\sqrt{a_p}),\n\\]\n\\[\ne^{o(T)}\n\\left(\\|F_T^{\\mathrm{odd}}\\|_{C^2}\n+\\sum_\\pm\\|H_T^\\pm\\|_{C^2}^2\\right)=o(A_T),\n\\]\n\\[\ne^{o(T)}(a_p/h_T+h_T^{16})=o(A_T).\n\\]\nThey respectively control the adaptive observer and connection, preserve the signed curvature signal, and contradict the test.</p><p>Both signed data converge in the prescribed finite seminorm and remain in \\(\\mathcal U\\). Therefore any relative neighborhood of a passing datum contains a failing datum, proving empty relative interior.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 2, "cite"], [3, 0, "cite"], [4, 0, "cite"], [4, 10, "cite"], [5, 24, "cite"], [6, 4, "cite"], [7, 4, "cite"], [8, 4, "cite"], [8, 11, "cite"], [9, 4, "cite"], [10, 14, "cite"], [10, 24, "cite"], [10, 32, "cite"], [11, 10, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [11, 13, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [13, 10, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [14, 16, "cite"], [14, 17, "cite"], [14, 18, "cite"], [14, 21, "cite"], [15, 18, "cite"], [15, 21, "cite"], [15, 25, "cite"], [16, 18, "cite"], [16, 19, "cite"], [16, 21, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [17, 21, "cite"], [17, 28, "cite"], [18, 1, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [18, 21, "cite"], [18, 27, "cite"], [18, 32, "cite"], [19, 1, "cite"], [19, 21, "cite"], [19, 23, "cite"], [19, 32, "cite"], [20, 19, "cite"], [21, 23, "cite"], [21, 24, "cite"], [21, 27, "cite"], [21, 32, "cite"], [22, 21, "cite"], [23, 24, "cite"], [23, 27, "cite"], [23, 32, "cite"], [24, 27, "cite"], [24, 29, "cite"], [24, 32, "cite"], [25, 24, "cite"], [25, 26, "cite"], [26, 24, "cite"], [27, 29, "cite"], [27, 32, "cite"], [28, 27, "cite"], [29, 32, "cite"], [30, 29, "cite"], [31, 32, "cite"], [32, 0, "cite"]]});
